-- Additional PHY 124 CBT questions mined from a full 300-page textbook the
-- user supplied (Electric Charge/Field/Potential through Nuclear Physics),
-- covering worked examples and end-of-chapter exercises across all 15
-- chapters. 186 rows appended to the existing 64-question bank.
-- Every answer independently verified with sympy; several genuine errors in
-- the source book's own worked examples were caught and corrected along the
-- way (see commit message for detail). Zero overlap with the existing bank.

insert into public.cbt_questions
  (subject_id, subject_name, institution, faculty, department, level,
   question, options, correct_index, explanation, topic, is_general,
   semester, units)
values
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'In a hydrogen atom, an electron and a proton are separated by a distance of $5.3\times10^{-11}\,m$. Taking the proton mass as $1.7\times10^{-27}\,kg$ and the electron mass as $9.1\times10^{-31}\,kg$, what is the order of magnitude of the ratio of the electrostatic force between them to the gravitational force between them?', '["$2.2\\times10^{39}$", "$2.2\\times10^{19}$", "$4.4\\times10^{39}$", "$2.2\\times10^{-39}$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Coulomb force: $F_e = k e^2/r^2 = (8.99\times10^9)(1.6\times10^{-19})^2/(5.3\times10^{-11})^2 \approx 8.2\times10^{-8}\,N$. Gravitational force: $F_g = G m_p m_e/r^2 = (6.67\times10^{-11})(1.7\times10^{-27})(9.1\times10^{-31})/(5.3\times10^{-11})^2 \approx 3.7\times10^{-47}\,N$. The ratio $F_e/F_g \approx 2.2\times10^{39}$, independent of the separation r since both forces obey an inverse-square law.', 'Electrostatics: Coulomb''s and Gauss''s laws', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'An alpha particle (charge $+2e$) and an electron (charge $-e$) are momentarily separated by $1.0\times10^{-13}\,m$ inside a nucleus. What is the magnitude of the electrostatic force between them?', '["$4.6\\times10^{-2}\\,N$", "$2.3\\times10^{-2}\\,N$", "$4.6\\times10^{-4}\\,N$", "$9.2\\times10^{-2}\\,N$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$F = k(2e)(e)/r^2 = (8.99\times10^9)(2)(1.6\times10^{-19})^2/(1.0\times10^{-13})^2 \approx 4.6\times10^{-2}\,N$. Forgetting the factor of 2 from the alpha particle''s charge gives the halved distractor; using $r$ instead of $r^2$ in the denominator gives the very large wrong-power distractor.', 'Electrostatics: Coulomb''s and Gauss''s laws', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'Two protons are separated by some distance r. Which expression correctly gives the ratio of the electrostatic force between them to the gravitational force between them, and what is its approximate value?', '["$\\dfrac{k e^2}{G m_p^2}$, approximately $1.2\\times10^{36}$", "$\\dfrac{k e^2}{G m_p^2}$, approximately $1.2\\times10^{18}$", "$\\dfrac{k e^2 r}{G m_p^2}$, approximately $1.2\\times10^{36}$", "$\\dfrac{G m_p^2}{k e^2}$, approximately $8.3\\times10^{-37}$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Both forces vary as $1/r^2$, so $r$ cancels in the ratio: $F_e/F_g = ke^2/(Gm_p^2) = (8.99\times10^9)(1.6\times10^{-19})^2 / [(6.67\times10^{-11})(1.7\times10^{-27})^2] \approx 1.2\times10^{36}$.', 'Electrostatics: Coulomb''s and Gauss''s laws', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'Point charges of $+88\,\mu C$, $-55\,\mu C$ and $+70\,\mu C$ are placed in that order along a straight line, with the middle charge exactly $0.75\,m$ from each of the other two. What is the magnitude of the net electrostatic force on the middle charge, and in which direction does it act?', '["About $15.8\\,N$, directed towards the $+88\\,\\mu C$ charge", "About $15.8\\,N$, directed towards the $+70\\,\\mu C$ charge", "About $36.9\\,N$, directed towards the $+88\\,\\mu C$ charge", "Zero, since the two forces on it cancel"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The $+88\,\mu C$ charge attracts the middle ($-55\,\mu C$) charge with force $k(88\times10^{-6})(55\times10^{-6})/(0.75)^2 \approx 51.7\,N$ pulling it towards the $+88\,\mu C$ charge, while the $+70\,\mu C$ charge attracts it with $k(70\times10^{-6})(55\times10^{-6})/(0.75)^2 \approx 41.1\,N$ pulling it the other way. Since these attractions act in opposite directions along the line, the net force is the difference, about $15.8\,N$, directed towards the stronger attractor, the $+88\,\mu C$ charge.', 'Electrostatics: Coulomb''s and Gauss''s laws', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A uniform electric field of magnitude $350\,N/C$ passes through a flat rectangular surface measuring $20\,cm$ by $15\,cm$. The field makes an angle of $50^{\circ}$ with the normal to the surface. What is the electric flux through the surface?', '["$6.75\\,N\\cdot m^2/C$", "$10.5\\,N\\cdot m^2/C$", "$8.04\\,N\\cdot m^2/C$", "$4.06\\,N\\cdot m^2/C$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Flux $\Phi = EA\cos\theta$, where $A = 0.20\,m \times 0.15\,m = 0.03\,m^2$. $\Phi = 350 \times 0.03 \times \cos(50^{\circ}) \approx 6.75\,N\cdot m^2/C$. Using the angle with the surface instead of the normal (i.e. $\sin 50^{\circ}$) gives the second distractor; omitting $\cos\theta$ entirely gives $10.5$.', 'Electrostatics: Coulomb''s and Gauss''s laws', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A solid sphere of radius R carries a uniform positive volume charge density. How does the magnitude of the electric field it produces vary with the distance r from its centre, for points inside ($r<R$) and outside ($r>R$) the sphere?', '["Increases linearly with r inside the sphere, then decreases as $1/r^2$ outside", "Is zero everywhere inside the sphere, then decreases as $1/r^2$ outside", "Increases linearly with r inside, then remains constant outside", "Decreases as $1/r^2$ inside, then increases linearly outside"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Applying Gauss''s law with a spherical Gaussian surface of radius $r<R$, the enclosed charge grows as $r^3$, giving $E = \dfrac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_0}\dfrac{Qr}{R^3}$, which rises linearly with r. For $r>R$ the entire charge Q is enclosed and the sphere behaves like a point charge, so $E = \dfrac{1}{4\pi\varepsilon_0}\dfrac{Q}{r^2}$, an inverse-square fall-off. The field is therefore continuous and reaches its maximum exactly at $r=R$.', 'Electrostatics: Coulomb''s and Gauss''s laws', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'An oil drop of radius $1.64\times10^{-6}\,m$ and density $8.51\times10^{2}\,kg/m^3$ is held stationary in a downward electric field of magnitude $1.92\times10^{5}\,N/C$ by an upward electrostatic force that exactly balances its weight. Taking $g = 9.8\,m/s^2$ and the elementary charge as $1.6\times10^{-19}\,C$, how many excess electrons does the drop carry?', '["5", "3", "8", "10"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Mass of the drop: $m = \rho \cdot \tfrac{4}{3}\pi r^3 \approx 1.57\times10^{-14}\,kg$. For equilibrium $qE = mg$, so $q = mg/E \approx 8.0\times10^{-19}\,C$. The number of electron charges is $N = q/e \approx 5$, one of the earliest direct pieces of evidence that charge is quantized.', 'Electrostatics: Coulomb''s and Gauss''s laws', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A charge of $+e$ sits at the centre of a hydrogen atom, $5.0\times10^{-11}\,m$ from the orbiting electron. What is the approximate magnitude of the electric field this proton produces at the electron''s location?', '["$5.8\\times10^{11}\\,N/C$", "$5.8\\times10^{9}\\,N/C$", "$2.9\\times10^{11}\\,N/C$", "$5.8\\times10^{13}\\,N/C$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$E = ke/r^2 = (8.99\times10^9)(1.6\times10^{-19})/(5.0\times10^{-11})^2 \approx 5.8\times10^{11}\,N/C$. Using $r$ instead of $r^2$ in the denominator would give an answer many orders of magnitude too small, while halving the charge (as if only $e/2$ were present) gives the second distractor.', 'Electrostatics: Coulomb''s and Gauss''s laws', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A small copper plate has a mass of $3\,g$. Copper has atomic number $Z=29$ and atomic mass $63.5\,g/mol$ (Avogadro''s number $=6.02\times10^{23}\,mol^{-1}$). What is the magnitude of the total charge of all the electrons contained in the plate?', '["$1.32\\times10^{5}\\,C$", "$4.55\\times10^{3}\\,C$", "$1.32\\times10^{5}\\,C$ but negative only for half the atoms", "$6.6\\times10^{4}\\,C$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Number of atoms: $N = \dfrac{3\,g}{63.5\,g/mol}\times6.02\times10^{23} \approx 2.84\times10^{22}$ atoms. Each atom contributes $Z=29$ electrons, so the total electron charge magnitude is $Q = N\times Z \times e = 2.84\times10^{22}\times29\times1.6\times10^{-19} \approx 1.32\times10^{5}\,C$. Forgetting to multiply by Z (using one electron per atom) gives the smaller distractor $4.55\times10^{3}\,C$.', 'Electric field and electrostatic potential', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'What is the magnitude of the electric field at a point $20\,cm$ from an isolated point charge of $20\,\mu C$, and what force would this field exert on a $5\,\mu C$ charge placed at that point?', '["$E \\approx 4.5\\times10^{6}\\,N/C$, $F \\approx 22.5\\,N$", "$E \\approx 4.5\\times10^{6}\\,N/C$, $F \\approx 90\\,N$", "$E \\approx 9.0\\times10^{5}\\,N/C$, $F \\approx 4.5\\,N$", "$E \\approx 2.25\\times10^{6}\\,N/C$, $F \\approx 11.25\\,N$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$E = kQ/r^2 = (8.99\times10^9)(20\times10^{-6})/(0.20)^2 \approx 4.5\times10^6\,N/C$. The force on the test charge is $F = qE = (5\times10^{-6})(4.5\times10^6) \approx 22.5\,N$. Doubling the test charge''s contribution (as if $q=10\,\mu C$) gives $90\,N$; halving E through a distance-instead-of-radius mistake gives the third distractor.', 'Electric field and electrostatic potential', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A tiny test charge of $5\,nC$ placed at a certain point experiences a force of $2\times10^{-4}\,N$ directed along the positive x-axis. What is the electric field at that point, and what force would it exert on an electron placed there?', '["$E = 4\\times10^{4}\\,N/C$ in the $+x$ direction; force on electron $\\approx 6.4\\times10^{-15}\\,N$ in the $-x$ direction", "$E = 4\\times10^{4}\\,N/C$ in the $+x$ direction; force on electron $\\approx 6.4\\times10^{-15}\\,N$ in the $+x$ direction", "$E = 2.5\\times10^{5}\\,N/C$ in the $+x$ direction; force on electron $\\approx 4.0\\times10^{-14}\\,N$ in the $-x$ direction", "$E = 4\\times10^{4}\\,N/C$ in the $-x$ direction; force on electron $\\approx 6.4\\times10^{-15}\\,N$ in the $+x$ direction"]'::jsonb, 0, '$E = F/q = (2\times10^{-4})/(5\times10^{-9}) = 4\times10^{4}\,N/C$, in the same direction as the force on the positive test charge, i.e. $+x$. The force on an electron is $F=eE=(1.6\times10^{-19})(4\times10^4)\approx6.4\times10^{-15}\,N$, but because the electron is negative this force points opposite to E, i.e. in the $-x$ direction. Dividing instead of the correct arithmetic (using $F/q$ with $q$ in microcoulombs) gives the $2.5\times10^5$ distractor.', 'Electric field and electrostatic potential', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A charge of $+8\,nC$ sits at the origin and a second charge of $+12\,nC$ sits on the x-axis at $x=4\,m$. What is the net electric field at a point P on the x-axis at $x=7\,m$?', '["About $13.5\\,N/C$, pointing in the $+x$ direction", "About $13.5\\,N/C$, pointing in the $-x$ direction", "About $6.7\\,N/C$, pointing in the $+x$ direction", "About $1.47\\,N/C$, pointing in the $+x$ direction"]'::jsonb, 0, 'P is $7\,m$ from the origin charge and $3\,m$ from the second charge. Both charges are positive, so both fields at P point away from their source, in the $+x$ direction: $E = \dfrac{k(8\times10^{-9})}{7^2} + \dfrac{k(12\times10^{-9})}{3^2} \approx 1.47 + 12.0 \approx 13.5\,N/C$. Using only the nearer charge''s contribution gives the $1.47$ distractor.', 'Electric field and electrostatic potential', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'Two positive point charges $q_1=8\,nC$ and $q_2=12\,nC$ lie on the x-axis at the origin and at $x=4\,m$ respectively. What is the approximate magnitude of the net electric field at a point Q on the y-axis at $y=3\,m$ (note that Q is $3\,m$ from $q_1$ and $5\,m$ from $q_2$)?', '["$\\approx 11.2\\,N/C$", "$\\approx 12.3\\,N/C$", "$\\approx 8.0\\,N/C$", "$\\approx 15.7\\,N/C$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$q_1$ produces a field of magnitude $E_1=kq_1/3^2\approx 8.0\,N/C$ directed purely along $+y$ (straight from the origin to Q). $q_2$ produces $E_2=kq_2/5^2\approx4.3\,N/C$ directed from $(4,0)$ to $(0,3)$, which has components $E_{2x}=-E_2(4/5)\approx-3.5\,N/C$ and $E_{2y}=E_2(3/5)\approx2.6\,N/C$. Adding components gives $E_x\approx-3.5\,N/C$, $E_y\approx10.6\,N/C$, and the resultant magnitude is $\sqrt{E_x^2+E_y^2}\approx11.2\,N/C$. Simply adding $E_1+E_2$ as scalars (ignoring direction) gives the $12.3\,N/C$ distractor.', 'Electric field and electrostatic potential', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A plastic rod is rubbed against a wool shirt and thereby acquires a charge of $-0.8\,\mu C$. Taking the elementary charge as $1.6\times10^{-19}\,C$, how many electrons were transferred from the wool shirt to the rod?', '["$5.0\\times10^{12}$", "$1.25\\times10^{5}$", "$5.0\\times10^{6}$", "$8.0\\times10^{13}$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$N = |Q|/e = (0.8\times10^{-6})/(1.6\times10^{-19}) = 5.0\times10^{12}$ electrons. Dividing charge by e without converting microcoulombs to coulombs would give an answer many orders of magnitude too small (the second distractor).', 'Electric field and electrostatic potential', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'Two equal charges $q_1=q_2=-6\,\mu C$ are fixed on the y-axis at $y=+3\,cm$ and $y=-3\,cm$. What is the magnitude of the net electric field at a point on the x-axis at $x=4\,cm$?', '["$\\approx 3.45\\times10^{7}\\,N/C$, pointing towards the y-axis", "$\\approx 3.45\\times10^{7}\\,N/C$, pointing away from the y-axis", "$\\approx 2.16\\times10^{7}\\,N/C$, pointing towards the y-axis", "$\\approx 1.73\\times10^{7}\\,N/C$, pointing towards the y-axis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Each charge is a distance $r=\sqrt{3^2+4^2}\,cm = 5\,cm$ from the field point, giving $E_{each}=k(6\times10^{-6})/(0.05)^2\approx2.16\times10^7\,N/C$. By symmetry the y-components cancel and the x-components add; since both charges are negative, each field points towards its source, so the x-components are $E_{each}\cos\theta$ with $\cos\theta=4/5$, each contributing back towards the y-axis. Adding the two: $E_{net}=2\times2.16\times10^7\times(4/5)\approx3.45\times10^7\,N/C$, directed towards the y-axis (the negative charges attract a positive test charge back towards them). Forgetting to include both charges gives the $2.16\times10^7$ distractor.', 'Electric field and electrostatic potential', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'Using the field found for two $-6\,\mu C$ charges fixed at $y=\pm3\,cm$ on the y-axis (net field magnitude $\approx3.45\times10^{7}\,N/C$ at $x=4\,cm$, pointing towards the y-axis), what force would this field exert on a $+2\,\mu C$ test charge placed at $x=4\,cm$?', '["$\\approx 69\\,N$, pulling the test charge towards the y-axis", "$\\approx 69\\,N$, pushing the test charge away from the y-axis", "$\\approx 34.5\\,N$, pulling the test charge towards the y-axis", "$\\approx 138\\,N$, pulling the test charge towards the y-axis"]'::jsonb, 0, '$F=qE=(2\times10^{-6})(3.45\times10^7)\approx69\,N$. Since the field points towards the y-axis (towards the attracting negative charges) and the test charge is positive, the force is in the same direction as the field, i.e. the positive test charge is pulled towards the y-axis. Using half the field (as computed from only one source charge) gives the $34.5\,N$ distractor.', 'Electric field and electrostatic potential', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'Three capacitors of $3\,\mu F$, $12\,\mu F$ and $4\,\mu F$ are connected in series across a $16\,V$ battery. What is the charge stored on each capacitor, and what is the potential difference across the $12\,\mu F$ capacitor?', '["Each stores $24\\,\\mu C$; the p.d. across the $12\\,\\mu F$ capacitor is $2\\,V$", "Each stores $24\\,\\mu C$; the p.d. across the $12\\,\\mu F$ capacitor is $8\\,V$", "Each stores $48\\,\\mu C$; the p.d. across the $12\\,\\mu F$ capacitor is $4\\,V$", "Each stores $16\\,\\mu C$; the p.d. across the $12\\,\\mu F$ capacitor is $1.33\\,V$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'For series capacitors, $1/C_{eq}=1/3+1/12+1/4$ (in $\mu F^{-1}$) gives $C_{eq}=1.5\,\mu F$. Series capacitors all carry the same charge as the equivalent: $Q=C_{eq}V=1.5\times16=24\,\mu C$. The voltage across the $12\,\mu F$ capacitor is $V=Q/C=24/12=2\,V$ (and indeed $8+2+6=16\,V$ across the three, as expected). Assuming the capacitors are in parallel (so each gets the full $16\,V$ and charge $=CV$) is a common error that does not match this series circuit.', 'Capacitance and dielectrics', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'In a network, a $1\,\mu F$ capacitor and a $5\,\mu F$ capacitor are connected in parallel; this combination is then connected in series with a $3\,\mu F$ capacitor, and a $4\,\mu F$ capacitor is connected in parallel with that whole series combination, across an $8\,V$ supply. What is the equivalent capacitance of the entire network?', '["$6\\,\\mu F$", "$8\\,\\mu F$", "$13\\,\\mu F$", "$2\\,\\mu F$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The $1\,\mu F$ and $5\,\mu F$ capacitors in parallel give $6\,\mu F$. This in series with the $3\,\mu F$ capacitor gives $1/C = 1/3+1/6 = 1/2$, so $C=2\,\mu F$. Finally, this $2\,\mu F$ in parallel with the $4\,\mu F$ capacitor gives $C_{eq}=2+4=6\,\mu F$. Simply adding all four values ($1+5+3+4=13$) as though they were all in parallel ignores the series branch.', 'Capacitance and dielectrics', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'For the network in the previous question (equivalent capacitance $6\,\mu F$ across an $8\,V$ supply, made of a $4\,\mu F$ capacitor in parallel with a series combination of $3\,\mu F$ and a parallel pair of $1\,\mu F$ and $5\,\mu F$), what charge is stored on the $4\,\mu F$ capacitor, and what is the potential difference across the $3\,\mu F$ capacitor?', '["$32\\,\\mu C$ on the $4\\,\\mu F$ capacitor; about $5.33\\,V$ across the $3\\,\\mu F$ capacitor", "$32\\,\\mu C$ on the $4\\,\\mu F$ capacitor; about $2.67\\,V$ across the $3\\,\\mu F$ capacitor", "$48\\,\\mu C$ on the $4\\,\\mu F$ capacitor; the full $8\\,V$ across the $3\\,\\mu F$ capacitor", "$16\\,\\mu C$ on the $4\\,\\mu F$ capacitor; about $5.33\\,V$ across the $3\\,\\mu F$ capacitor"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The $4\,\mu F$ capacitor is directly across the $8\,V$ supply, so $Q_4=C_4V=4\times8=32\,\mu C$. The series branch (equivalent $2\,\mu F$) also has the full $8\,V$ across it, storing $Q=2\times8=16\,\mu C$ — the same charge flows through the $3\,\mu F$ capacitor, so its p.d. is $V=Q/C=16/3\approx5.33\,V$ (leaving $8-5.33\approx2.67\,V$ across the parallel $1\,\mu F$/$5\,\mu F$ pair).', 'Capacitance and dielectrics', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A $2.0\,\mu F$ capacitor is charged by a $12\,V$ battery and then disconnected from it. It is then connected directly in parallel with an identical but uncharged $5.0\,\mu F$ capacitor. What is the total energy stored in the two capacitors (a) before and (b) after they are connected together?', '["(a) $1.44\\times10^{-4}\\,J$, (b) about $4.1\\times10^{-5}\\,J$", "(a) $1.44\\times10^{-4}\\,J$, (b) $1.44\\times10^{-4}\\,J$ (energy is conserved)", "(a) $2.4\\times10^{-4}\\,J$, (b) about $4.1\\times10^{-5}\\,J$", "(a) $1.44\\times10^{-4}\\,J$, (b) about $8.2\\times10^{-5}\\,J$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Before connecting: $U=\tfrac12CV^2=\tfrac12(2\times10^{-6})(12)^2=1.44\times10^{-4}\,J$. The charge on the first capacitor is $Q=CV=24\,\mu C$; once connected to the uncharged capacitor, this same charge redistributes over $C_{eq}=2+5=7\,\mu F$, giving a common final voltage $V_f=Q/C_{eq}\approx3.43\,V$. The new stored energy is $U=\tfrac12C_{eq}V_f^2\approx4.1\times10^{-5}\,J$ — noticeably less than before, the difference being dissipated as heat and radiation as charge flows between the plates. Energy is not conserved in this irreversible charge-sharing process even though charge is.', 'Capacitance and dielectrics', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A wire of length $20.0\,m$ and diameter $1.50\,mm$ has a resistance of $2.50\,\Omega$. What is the resistance of a wire made from the same material but with a diameter of $3.00\,mm$ and a length of $35.0\,m$?', '["$\\approx 1.10\\,\\Omega$", "$\\approx 4.38\\,\\Omega$", "$\\approx 0.55\\,\\Omega$", "$\\approx 2.19\\,\\Omega$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'First find the resistivity: $\rho = RA/L$ with $A=\pi(0.75\times10^{-3})^2\approx1.77\times10^{-6}\,m^2$, giving $\rho\approx2.2\times10^{-7}\,\Omega m$. For the new wire, $A''=\pi(1.5\times10^{-3})^2\approx7.07\times10^{-6}\,m^2$, so $R''=\rho L''/A'' \approx (2.2\times10^{-7})(35.0)/(7.07\times10^{-6})\approx1.10\,\Omega$. Using the old cross-sectional area instead of the quadrupled one (since diameter doubled) gives the $4.38\,\Omega$ distractor.', 'Electric current, Ohm''s law and circuits', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A wire with resistance $6.0\,\Omega$ is drawn out through a die so that its length becomes three times its original length, while its volume (and the resistivity of the material) stays unchanged. What is the resistance of the longer, thinner wire?', '["$54\\,\\Omega$", "$18\\,\\Omega$", "$2.0\\,\\Omega$", "$6.0\\,\\Omega$ (unchanged)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Since volume $=A L$ is constant, tripling the length means the new cross-sectional area is $A/3$. Resistance $R=\rho L/A$, so $R_2 = \rho(3L)/(A/3) = 9\rho L/A = 9R_1 = 9\times6.0=54\,\Omega$. Forgetting to also shrink the area (i.e. assuming $R\propto L$ only) gives the $18\,\Omega$ distractor.', 'Electric current, Ohm''s law and circuits', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A silver wire has a length of $10\,m$ and a cross-sectional area of $6\,mm^2$. Given that the conductivity of silver is $5.8\times10^{7}\,(\Omega m)^{-1}$, what is the resistance of the wire at $20^{\circ}C$, and what current flows through it when a potential difference of $20\,V$ is maintained across it?', '["$R\\approx0.0287\\,\\Omega$, $I\\approx696\\,A$", "$R\\approx2.87\\,\\Omega$, $I\\approx6.96\\,A$", "$R\\approx0.0287\\,\\Omega$, $I\\approx0.696\\,A$", "$R\\approx0.172\\,\\Omega$, $I\\approx116\\,A$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Resistivity is the reciprocal of conductivity: $\rho=1/\sigma\approx1.72\times10^{-8}\,\Omega m$. $R=\rho L/A = (1.72\times10^{-8})(10)/(6\times10^{-6})\approx0.0287\,\Omega$. By Ohm''s law, $I=V/R=20/0.0287\approx696\,A$ (a large current because the resistance is very small for such a thick, short conductor). Forgetting to convert $mm^2$ to $m^2$ gives resistance $1000\times$ too large and the second distractor''s current.', 'Electric current, Ohm''s law and circuits', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'The silver wire above has resistance $0.0287\,\Omega$ at $20^{\circ}C$. If silver''s temperature coefficient of resistance is $3.9\times10^{-3}\,{}^{\circ}C^{-1}$, what is its resistivity at $100^{\circ}C$?', '["$\\approx2.26\\times10^{-8}\\,\\Omega m$", "$\\approx1.72\\times10^{-8}\\,\\Omega m$ (unchanged)", "$\\approx1.98\\times10^{-8}\\,\\Omega m$", "$\\approx5.37\\times10^{-8}\\,\\Omega m$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$\rho_{100} = \rho_{20}\left[1+\alpha(T-T_0)\right] = (1.72\times10^{-8})\left[1+(3.9\times10^{-3})(100-20)\right] \approx2.26\times10^{-8}\,\Omega m$. Applying the temperature correction to the resistance instead and reporting it as if it were the resistivity, or using $(T-T_0)=100$ instead of $80$, are common sources of error here.', 'Electric current, Ohm''s law and circuits', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A tungsten wire has a resistance of $1.50\,\Omega$ at room temperature ($20^{\circ}C$). Taking tungsten''s temperature coefficient of resistance as $0.0045\,{}^{\circ}C^{-1}$, what is its resistance at $1000^{\circ}C$?', '["$\\approx8.12\\,\\Omega$", "$\\approx2.04\\,\\Omega$", "$\\approx6.75\\,\\Omega$", "$\\approx1.50\\,\\Omega$ (unchanged)"]'::jsonb, 0, '$R = R_0\left[1+\alpha(T-T_0)\right] = 1.50\left[1+(0.0045)(1000-20)\right] \approx8.12\,\Omega$. Using $(1000-20)$ but forgetting the initial $1.50\,\Omega$ multiplier, or mistakenly using a final temperature of only $100^{\circ}C$ (giving $2.04\,\Omega$), are the sources of the distractors.', 'Electric current, Ohm''s law and circuits', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'An X-ray tube operating at $80\,kV$ carries a current of $7.0\,mA$. What electrical power does the tube consume?', '["$560\\,W$", "$5.6\\,W$", "$11.4\\,W$", "$5600\\,W$"]'::jsonb, 0, '$P=IV=(7.0\times10^{-3})(80\times10^{3})=560\,W$. Mixing up powers of ten when converting milliamps or kilovolts produces the other distractors.', 'Electric current, Ohm''s law and circuits', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A copper cable is designed to carry a maximum current of $40\,A$ without dissipating more than $1.8\,W$ of heat per metre of its length. Taking the resistivity of copper as $1.67\times10^{-8}\,\Omega m$, what minimum diameter must the cable have?', '["$\\approx4.3\\,mm$", "$\\approx2.2\\,mm$", "$\\approx8.6\\,mm$", "$\\approx1.5\\,mm$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Power dissipated per unit length is $P/L = I^2\rho/A$, so the minimum area is $A = I^2\rho/(P/L) = (40)^2(1.67\times10^{-8})/1.8 \approx1.48\times10^{-5}\,m^2$. The diameter is $d=\sqrt{4A/\pi}\approx4.3\times10^{-3}\,m = 4.3\,mm$. Using the current instead of its square, or forgetting the factor of 4 in $A=\pi d^2/4$, produces the other distractors.', 'Electric current, Ohm''s law and circuits', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'An electric fan draws a steady current of $2.0\,A$ from a $220\,V$ mains supply. What is its power rating, its effective resistance, and how much would it cost (in Naira) to run the fan continuously for a $30$-day month if electricity costs $8$ kobo per $kWh$?', '["$440\\,W$; $110\\,\\Omega$; about $\\text{\\textnaira}25.34$", "$440\\,W$; $110\\,\\Omega$; about $\\text{\\textnaira}2.53$", "$110\\,W$; $440\\,\\Omega$; about $\\text{\\textnaira}25.34$", "$440\\,W$; $220\\,\\Omega$; about $\\text{\\textnaira}12.67$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Power: $P=IV=2.0\times220=440\,W$. Resistance: $R=V/I=220/2.0=110\,\Omega$. Energy used in 30 days: $E = P\times t = 0.440\,kW \times (24\times30)\,h = 316.8\,kWh$. Cost $=316.8\times8=2534.4$ kobo $\approx\text{\textnaira}25.34$. Misplacing a decimal point in the kobo-to-Naira conversion gives the $\text{\textnaira}2.53$ distractor.', 'Electric current, Ohm''s law and circuits', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'In an experiment, a student needs to limit the current flowing through a circuit to $20\,A$ from a $240\,V$ supply. A $10\,\Omega$ resistor is already in the circuit. What auxiliary resistance, connected in series, should be added?', '["$2\\,\\Omega$", "$12\\,\\Omega$", "$22\\,\\Omega$", "$4.8\\,\\Omega$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The total resistance required is $R_{total}=V/I=240/20=12\,\Omega$. Since a $10\,\Omega$ resistor is already present and resistors in series add directly, the auxiliary resistor needed is $R_{aux}=12-10=2\,\Omega$. Forgetting to subtract the existing resistor gives $12\,\Omega$; adding instead of computing the required total gives $22\,\Omega$.', 'Electric current, Ohm''s law and circuits', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'Three resistors of $2\,\Omega$, $6\,\Omega$ and $9\,\Omega$ are first connected in series, and separately connected in parallel, each combination across a $120\,V$ supply. What is the current drawn from the supply in each case?', '["Series: $\\approx7.06\\,A$; Parallel: $\\approx93.3\\,A$", "Series: $\\approx7.06\\,A$; Parallel: $\\approx7.06\\,A$", "Series: $\\approx93.3\\,A$; Parallel: $\\approx7.06\\,A$", "Series: $\\approx17.0\\,A$; Parallel: $\\approx1.29\\,A$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'In series, $R_{eq}=2+6+9=17\,\Omega$, so $I=120/17\approx7.06\,A$. In parallel, $1/R_{eq}=1/2+1/6+1/9$, giving $R_{eq}\approx1.29\,\Omega$, and the current drawn from the supply is the sum of the branch currents, $I=120/2+120/6+120/9=60+20+13.3\approx93.3\,A$. Swapping which configuration gives the larger current is the error behind the third distractor — a parallel combination always has lower equivalent resistance and therefore draws more total current than the same resistors in series.', 'Electric current, Ohm''s law and circuits', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A galvanometer has a coil resistance of $5\,\Omega$ and gives full-scale deflection for a current of $10\,mA$. What value of shunt resistance, connected in parallel with the coil, is needed to convert it into an ammeter reading up to $10.0\,A$?', '["$\\approx5.0\\times10^{-3}\\,\\Omega$", "$\\approx5.0\\,\\Omega$", "$\\approx0.5\\,\\Omega$", "$\\approx2.5\\times10^{-3}\\,\\Omega$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The shunt must carry the remaining current $I_S = I_{total}-I_G = 10.0-0.010=9.99\,A$, and since shunt and coil share the same voltage, $I_S R_S = I_G R_G$, giving $R_S = I_G R_G/I_S = (0.010)(5)/9.99 \approx5.0\times10^{-3}\,\Omega$. Forgetting to subtract the small galvanometer current from the total (using $I_S=10.0\,A$) barely changes the answer, but using $R_S=I_{total}R_G/I_G$ instead (inverting the ratio) gives the $5.0\,\Omega$ distractor, which would make an extremely insensitive ammeter.', 'Electric current, Ohm''s law and circuits', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'In a potentiometer experiment, a standard cell of known emf $1.5\,V$ gives a balance length of $60.0\,cm$. When it is replaced by a cell of unknown emf, the balance length becomes $80.0\,cm$. What is the emf of the unknown cell?', '["$2.0\\,V$", "$1.125\\,V$", "$1.5\\,V$", "$2.67\\,V$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Since a potentiometer''s balance length is directly proportional to the emf being measured (for a constant driver current), $E_2/E_1 = l_2/l_1$, so $E_2 = 1.5\times(80.0/60.0) = 2.0\,V$. Inverting the length ratio gives the $1.125\,V$ distractor.', 'Electric current, Ohm''s law and circuits', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'In a metre-bridge experiment, a known resistance of $30\,\Omega$ is placed in one gap and an unknown resistance R in the other. The bridge balances when the jockey is $45\,cm$ from the end corresponding to the known resistance (so $55\,cm$ from the end corresponding to R). What is the value of R?', '["$\\approx36.7\\,\\Omega$", "$\\approx24.5\\,\\Omega$", "$\\approx30\\,\\Omega$", "$\\approx13.5\\,\\Omega$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'For a metre bridge, the ratio of the resistances equals the ratio of the corresponding balance lengths: $R/30 = 55/45$, so $R = 30\times55/45 \approx36.7\,\Omega$. Inverting the length ratio (using $45/55$ instead) gives the $24.5\,\Omega$ distractor.', 'Electric current, Ohm''s law and circuits', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A capacitor is charging through a resistor from a battery in a simple RC circuit. Approximately how many time constants ($RC$) must elapse before the capacitor''s voltage rises to within $1\%$ of its final equilibrium value?', '["About $4.6\\,RC$", "About $1.0\\,RC$", "About $0.99\\,RC$", "About $100\\,RC$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The charging equation is $Q=CE(1-e^{-t/RC})$, so reaching $99\%$ of the final value requires $0.99=1-e^{-t/RC}$, i.e. $e^{-t/RC}=0.01$, so $t/RC=\ln(100)\approx4.6$. Confusing the $63\%$-in-one-time-constant rule with the $99\%$ condition, or taking $t/RC$ as simply $0.99$, leads to the other distractors.', 'Electric current, Ohm''s law and circuits', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'Three identical resistors, each of $100\,\Omega$, must each be limited to a maximum power dissipation of $25\,W$. What is the maximum voltage that could be applied across the whole combination if the three resistors are connected (i) in series and (ii) in parallel?', '["Series: $150\\,V$; Parallel: $50\\,V$", "Series: $50\\,V$; Parallel: $150\\,V$", "Series: $150\\,V$; Parallel: $150\\,V$", "Series: $50\\,V$; Parallel: $50\\,V$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'For any $100\,\Omega$ resistor, $25\,W$ corresponds to a maximum current $I_{max}=\sqrt{P/R}=\sqrt{25/100}=0.5\,A$. In series, all three resistors carry this same current, so the maximum terminal voltage is $V=I_{max}\times(3\times100)=0.5\times300=150\,V$. In parallel, each resistor independently reaches its $25\,W$ limit when the voltage across it (and hence across the whole parallel combination) is $V=I_{max}R=0.5\times100=50\,V$. Series combinations therefore tolerate a much higher terminal voltage before any individual resistor is overloaded.', 'Electric current, Ohm''s law and circuits', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A battery with emf $6.0\,V$ and internal resistance $0.50\,\Omega$ is connected across an external resistor of $40\,\Omega$. What is the terminal voltage of the battery?', '["$\\approx5.93\\,V$", "$6.0\\,V$", "$\\approx5.26\\,V$", "$\\approx0.074\\,V$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The current drawn is $I=\dfrac{\varepsilon}{R+r}=\dfrac{6.0}{40+0.50}\approx0.148\,A$. The terminal voltage is $V=\varepsilon-Ir=6.0-(0.148)(0.50)\approx5.93\,V$, slightly less than the emf because of the internal resistance''s voltage drop. Ignoring internal resistance entirely gives the $6.0\,V$ distractor.', 'Electric current, Ohm''s law and circuits', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A straight wire of length $240\,m$ is stretched between two towers and carries a current of $150\,A$. The Earth''s magnetic field at that location has a magnitude of $5\times10^{-5}\,T$ and makes an angle of $60^{\circ}$ with the wire. What is the magnitude of the magnetic force on the wire?', '["$\\approx1.56\\,N$", "$\\approx1.80\\,N$", "$\\approx0.90\\,N$", "$\\approx3.12\\,N$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The force on a current-carrying wire in a magnetic field is $F=BIL\sin\theta$, where $\theta$ is the angle between the wire and the field. $F = (5\times10^{-5})(150)(240)\sin(60^{\circ}) \approx1.56\,N$. Using $\cos60^{\circ}$ instead of $\sin60^{\circ}$ gives the $0.90\,N$ distractor, while omitting the angle factor entirely gives $1.80\,N$.', 'Magnetic effects of currents', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'An electron moving through a magnetic field $B=0.72\,T$ directed along the z-axis experiences a magnetic force $\vec F = (3.2\hat i - 2.7\hat j)\times10^{-13}\,N$. Taking the electron''s charge as $-1.6\times10^{-19}\,C$, what are the x- and y-components of its velocity?', '["$v_x\\approx-2.34\\times10^{6}\\,m/s$, $v_y\\approx-2.78\\times10^{6}\\,m/s$", "$v_x\\approx2.34\\times10^{6}\\,m/s$, $v_y\\approx2.78\\times10^{6}\\,m/s$", "$v_x\\approx-2.78\\times10^{6}\\,m/s$, $v_y\\approx-2.34\\times10^{6}\\,m/s$", "$v_x\\approx-1.17\\times10^{6}\\,m/s$, $v_y\\approx-1.39\\times10^{6}\\,m/s$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'For $\vec F=q\vec v\times\vec B$ with $\vec B=B\hat k$ and $\vec v=v_x\hat i+v_y\hat j$, expanding the cross product gives $F_x=qv_yB$ and $F_y=-qv_xB$. Solving with the electron''s negative charge: $v_y=F_x/(qB)=(3.2\times10^{-13})/[(-1.6\times10^{-19})(0.72)]\approx-2.78\times10^{6}\,m/s$, and $v_x=-F_y/(qB)=-(-2.7\times10^{-13})/[(-1.6\times10^{-19})(0.72)]\approx-2.34\times10^{6}\,m/s$. Using the electron''s charge as positive (forgetting the sign) flips both components, giving the second distractor.', 'Magnetic effects of currents', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A uniform electric field of magnitude $2000\,N/C$ points along the negative y-direction. An electron enters this region moving along the x-direction (perpendicular to the field) with a speed of $1.0\times10^{6}\,m/s$. How far has the electron been deflected transverse to its original path by the time it has travelled $1.0\,cm$ along x?', '["$\\approx1.76\\,cm$", "$\\approx3.51\\,cm$", "$\\approx0.88\\,cm$", "$\\approx17.6\\,cm$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The transverse acceleration is $a = eE/m_e = (1.6\times10^{-19})(2000)/(9.11\times10^{-31}) \approx3.51\times10^{14}\,m/s^2$, directed opposite the field since the electron is negative. The time to cross $1.0\,cm$ at constant horizontal speed is $t = x/v_0 = 0.01/(1.0\times10^6) = 1.0\times10^{-8}\,s$. The transverse deflection is then $y=\tfrac12 a t^2 \approx1.76\,cm$. Leaving out the factor of $\tfrac12$ gives the $3.51\,cm$ distractor; misplacing a decimal in the cm/m conversion gives $17.6\,cm$.', 'Motion of a charged particle in electric and magnetic fields; Hall effect', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'An electron is fired directly against the field lines of a uniform electric field of magnitude $2000\,N/C$ (i.e. with its velocity antiparallel to the electric force on it), with an initial speed of $1.0\times10^{6}\,m/s$. Taking the electron''s charge as $1.6\times10^{-19}\,C$ and its mass as $9.11\times10^{-31}\,kg$, how far does it travel before the field brings it momentarily to rest?', '["$\\approx0.142\\,cm$", "$\\approx0.57\\,cm$", "$\\approx0.285\\,cm$", "$\\approx1.76\\,cm$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The electron decelerates uniformly under $a = eE/m_e = (1.6\times10^{-19})(2000)/(9.11\times10^{-31}) \approx3.51\times10^{14}\,m/s^2$. Using $v^2=v_0^2-2aX$ with $v=0$ gives $X = v_0^2/(2a) = (1.0\times10^6)^2/(2\times3.51\times10^{14})\approx1.42\times10^{-3}\,m\approx0.142\,cm$. Omitting the factor of 2 in the denominator gives $0.285\,cm$; mistakenly doubling the stated initial speed to $2.0\times10^{6}\,m/s$ before substituting gives $0.57\,cm$.', 'Motion of a charged particle in electric and magnetic fields; Hall effect', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'The HCl molecule has a dipole moment of about $3.4\times10^{-30}\,C\,m$, with the two atoms separated by about $1.0\times10^{-10}\,m$. What is the maximum torque this dipole can experience in a uniform electric field of magnitude $2.5\times10^{4}\,N/C$?', '["$\\approx8.5\\times10^{-26}\\,N\\,m$", "$\\approx6.0\\times10^{-26}\\,N\\,m$", "$\\approx1.7\\times10^{-25}\\,N\\,m$", "$\\approx4.25\\times10^{-26}\\,N\\,m$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Torque on a dipole is $\tau = PE\sin\theta$, which is maximised when $\sin\theta=1$ (dipole moment perpendicular to the field), giving $\tau_{max}=PE=(3.4\times10^{-30})(2.5\times10^{4}) \approx8.5\times10^{-26}\,N\,m$. Evaluating $PE\sin45^{\circ}$ instead of taking the true maximum gives the $6.0\times10^{-26}$ distractor; doubling or halving $P$ produces the other two.', 'Electric field and electrostatic potential', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'The HCl molecule (dipole moment $\approx3.4\times10^{-30}\,C\,m$) sits in a uniform electric field of magnitude $2.5\times10^{4}\,N/C$. How much work must be done on the molecule to rotate it $45^{\circ}$ away from its equilibrium orientation (aligned with the field)?', '["$\\approx2.49\\times10^{-26}\\,J$", "$\\approx8.5\\times10^{-26}\\,J$", "$\\approx6.0\\times10^{-26}\\,J$", "$\\approx1.25\\times10^{-26}\\,J$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The work needed is $W = PE(1-\cos\theta)$ with $\theta_1=0$ at equilibrium and $\theta=45^{\circ}$: $W=(3.4\times10^{-30})(2.5\times10^{4})(1-\cos45^{\circ})\approx2.49\times10^{-26}\,J$. Using $\theta=90^{\circ}$ instead of $45^{\circ}$ gives $PE\approx8.5\times10^{-26}\,J$; dropping the $(1-\cos\theta)$ structure and computing $PE\cos45^{\circ}$ directly gives $\approx6.0\times10^{-26}\,J$.', 'Electric field and electrostatic potential', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A proton moving at $2.0 \times 10^6$ m/s enters a uniform magnetic field of $0.5$ T directed perpendicular to its velocity. What is the radius of its circular path? (mass of proton $= 1.67 \times 10^{-27}$ kg, charge $= 1.6 \times 10^{-19}$ C)', '["0.0418 m", "0.0209 m", "0.0836 m", "0.167 m"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The magnetic force provides the centripetal force: $qvB = \frac{mv^2}{r}$, so $r = \frac{mv}{qB} = \frac{(1.67\times10^{-27})(2.0\times10^6)}{(1.6\times10^{-19})(0.5)} \approx 0.0418$ m. Halving this (0.0209 m) would come from mistakenly using $r=\frac{mv}{2qB}$, doubling it (0.0836 m) from using $r=\frac{2mv}{qB}$, and 0.167 m from forgetting to divide by q entirely.', 'Motion of a charged particle in electric and magnetic fields; Hall effect', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'An electron circles in a plane perpendicular to a uniform magnetic field of $0.02$ T. What is the period of one complete revolution? (electron mass $= 9.11\times10^{-31}$ kg, charge $= 1.6\times10^{-19}$ C)', '["$1.79\\times10^{-9}$ s", "$3.58\\times10^{-9}$ s", "$8.95\\times10^{-10}$ s", "$1.79\\times10^{-7}$ s"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The cyclotron period is independent of speed: $T = \frac{2\pi m}{qB} = \frac{2\pi(9.11\times10^{-31})}{(1.6\times10^{-19})(0.02)} \approx 1.79\times10^{-9}$ s. Doubling it (using $4\pi m/qB$) or halving it (using $\pi m/qB$) are common algebra slips, and $1.79\times10^{-7}$ s results from mistakenly using $B=0.02\times10^{-2}$ T.', 'Motion of a charged particle in electric and magnetic fields; Hall effect', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A copper strip of thickness $1.0$ mm carries a current of $5.0$ A in a magnetic field of $0.8$ T applied perpendicular to its flat face. Given that the free-electron density in copper is $8.5\times10^{28}\ \text{m}^{-3}$, what is the Hall voltage developed across the strip?', '["$2.94\\times10^{-7}$ V", "$5.88\\times10^{-7}$ V", "$1.47\\times10^{-7}$ V", "$2.94\\times10^{-4}$ V"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Hall voltage is $V_H = \frac{IB}{nqt} = \frac{(5.0)(0.8)}{(8.5\times10^{28})(1.6\times10^{-19})(1.0\times10^{-3})} \approx 2.94\times10^{-7}$ V. Doubling or halving this comes from misplacing a factor of 2, while $2.94\times10^{-4}$ V results from using the thickness in metres as if it were in millimetres (i.e. forgetting the $10^{-3}$ conversion).', 'Motion of a charged particle in electric and magnetic fields; Hall effect', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'In a velocity selector, a charged particle travels undeflected through a region where a uniform electric field of $3.0\times10^5$ V/m is perpendicular to a uniform magnetic field of $0.15$ T. What speed must the particle have?', '["$2.0\\times10^6$ m/s", "$4.5\\times10^4$ m/s", "$1.0\\times10^6$ m/s", "$4.0\\times10^6$ m/s"]'::jsonb, 0, 'For the electric and magnetic forces to balance, $qE = qvB$, so $v = E/B = \frac{3.0\times10^5}{0.15} = 2.0\times10^6$ m/s. Multiplying E by B instead of dividing gives $4.5\times10^4$ m/s, and the other options come from using half or double the correct ratio.', 'Motion of a charged particle in electric and magnetic fields; Hall effect', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A proton starting from rest is accelerated through a potential difference of 2000 V and then enters a uniform magnetic field of 0.6 T perpendicular to its velocity. What is the radius of its circular path in the field?', '["0.0108 m", "0.0215 m", "0.0054 m", "0.108 m"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The accelerating potential gives the proton kinetic energy $eV = \frac{1}{2}mv^2$, so $v = \sqrt{2eV/m} \approx 6.19\times10^5$ m/s. The radius is then $r = \frac{mv}{eB} = \frac{(1.67\times10^{-27})(6.19\times10^5)}{(1.6\times10^{-19})(0.6)} \approx 0.0108$ m. Doubling this (0.0215 m) comes from forgetting the factor of 2 under the square root for v, while 0.108 m comes from a misplaced decimal in B.', 'Motion of a charged particle in electric and magnetic fields; Hall effect', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A proton moving at $1.0\times10^6$ m/s enters a uniform magnetic field of $0.4$ T at an angle of $30^{\circ}$ to the field direction, tracing a helical path. What is the pitch of the helix (the distance travelled parallel to the field in one revolution)?', '["0.142 m", "0.0817 m", "0.284 m", "0.0653 m"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Only the velocity component perpendicular to $B$, $v_\perp = v\sin30^\circ$, determines the circular motion, while $v_\parallel = v\cos30^\circ$ carries the particle along the field. The period is $T=\frac{2\pi m}{qB}\approx1.64\times10^{-7}$ s (independent of angle), so the pitch is $v_\parallel T \approx (8.66\times10^5)(1.64\times10^{-7}) \approx 0.142$ m. Using $v\sin30^\circ$ instead of $v\cos30^\circ$ for the pitch gives 0.0817 m, a common mix-up of the parallel and perpendicular components.', 'Motion of a charged particle in electric and magnetic fields; Hall effect', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'Two singly-ionized isotopes of chlorine, of mass numbers 35 and 37, are each accelerated from rest through the same potential difference before entering a uniform magnetic field in a mass spectrometer. What is the ratio of the radius of the heavier ion''s path to that of the lighter ion''s path?', '["1.028", "1.057", "1.014", "0.973"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Since both ions gain the same kinetic energy $qV$ from the accelerating voltage, $v=\sqrt{2qV/m}$, and the path radius is $r=\frac{mv}{qB}=\frac{\sqrt{2mV/q}}{B}\propto\sqrt{m}$. So the ratio of radii is $\sqrt{37/35}\approx1.028$. Using $r\propto m$ instead (correct only when the ions have equal speed rather than equal accelerating voltage) gives the mass ratio itself, $37/35\approx1.057$, a common mix-up between constant-speed and constant-energy scaling.', 'Motion of a charged particle in electric and magnetic fields; Hall effect', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A charged particle moves in a uniform magnetic field along a circular path. What is the work done by the magnetic force on the particle during one complete revolution?', '["Zero, because the magnetic force is always perpendicular to the velocity", "Positive, because the particle speeds up", "Negative, because the field opposes the motion", "It depends on the radius of the circular path"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The magnetic force on a moving charge, $\vec{F} = q\vec{v}\times\vec{B}$, is always perpendicular to the velocity. Since work is $\vec{F}\cdot\vec{d}$, a force perpendicular to the displacement at every instant does zero work, so the particle''s speed (and kinetic energy) never changes, no matter the radius or number of revolutions.', 'Motion of a charged particle in electric and magnetic fields; Hall effect', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'At a certain location, the horizontal component of the Earth''s magnetic field is $1.8\times10^{-5}$ T, and the angle of dip (inclination) is $66^{\circ}$. What is the vertical component of the Earth''s field at that location?', '["$4.04\\times10^{-5}$ T", "$1.98\\times10^{-5}$ T", "$7.34\\times10^{-6}$ T", "$1.8\\times10^{-5}$ T"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The vertical and horizontal components are related to the angle of dip by $\tan(\text{dip}) = B_v/B_h$, so $B_v = B_h\tan(66^\circ) \approx (1.8\times10^{-5})(2.246) \approx 4.04\times10^{-5}$ T. Using $\sin$ instead of $\tan$ gives $1.98\times10^{-5}$ T, using $\cos$ gives roughly the same order but the wrong ratio ($7.34\times10^{-6}$ T if $B_h\cos66^\circ/\sin66^\circ$ is confused), and assuming $B_v=B_h$ ignores the dip angle entirely.', 'Permanent magnetism and the Earth''s field', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'At a location where the horizontal component of the Earth''s magnetic field is $1.8\times10^{-5}$ T and the vertical component is $4.04\times10^{-5}$ T, what is the magnitude of the total (resultant) magnetic field of the Earth at that point?', '["$4.43\\times10^{-5}$ T", "$5.84\\times10^{-5}$ T", "$2.24\\times10^{-5}$ T", "$4.04\\times10^{-5}$ T"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The total field is the vector sum of the perpendicular horizontal and vertical components: $B = \sqrt{B_h^2+B_v^2} = \sqrt{(1.8\times10^{-5})^2+(4.04\times10^{-5})^2} \approx 4.43\times10^{-5}$ T. Adding the components directly instead of combining them as a vector sum gives $5.84\times10^{-5}$ T, subtracting gives $2.24\times10^{-5}$ T, and taking only the vertical component ignores the horizontal contribution.', 'Permanent magnetism and the Earth''s field', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'At the magnetic equator, the angle of dip is approximately', '["$0^{\\circ}$, since the field there is entirely horizontal", "$90^{\\circ}$, since the field there is entirely vertical", "$45^{\\circ}$, midway between the two magnetic poles", "It varies randomly and cannot be predicted"]'::jsonb, 0, 'At the magnetic equator, the Earth''s field lines run parallel to the surface, so the field is purely horizontal and the vertical component is zero. Since $\tan(\text{dip})=B_v/B_h=0$, the angle of dip is $0^{\circ}$. A dip of $90^{\circ}$ (field purely vertical) instead occurs at the magnetic poles.', 'Permanent magnetism and the Earth''s field', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A small compass needle is placed at a neutral point near a bar magnet, where the magnet''s field exactly cancels the horizontal component of the Earth''s magnetic field. What can be said about the compass needle at this point?', '["It can point in any direction, since the net horizontal field there is zero", "It will always point due north, following the Earth''s field alone", "It will always point along the magnet''s axis", "It will spin continuously and never settle"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A neutral point is where the magnet''s field exactly cancels the Earth''s horizontal field, so the net horizontal magnetic field at that point is zero. With no net field to align it, the compass needle experiences no restoring torque and can rest in any orientation, rather than settling along a preferred direction.', 'Permanent magnetism and the Earth''s field', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'Which of the following best describes why the Earth''s magnetic field is useful for navigation using a simple compass?', '["The needle aligns with the horizontal component of the Earth''s field, pointing approximately toward magnetic north", "The needle is attracted to the geographic North Pole by gravity", "The needle aligns with the vertical component of the field only", "The needle points toward the nearest large iron ore deposit only"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A compass needle is a small freely-pivoting magnet that experiences a torque aligning it with the local magnetic field direction. Since the needle can only rotate horizontally, it responds to the horizontal component of the Earth''s field, settling approximately along the magnetic north-south direction, which is why it is useful for navigation.', 'Permanent magnetism and the Earth''s field', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A long straight wire carries a current of 15 A. What is the magnitude of the magnetic field at a perpendicular distance of 2.0 cm from the wire?', '["$1.5\\times10^{-4}$ T", "$3.0\\times10^{-4}$ T", "$7.5\\times10^{-5}$ T", "$1.5\\times10^{-6}$ T"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The field around a long straight wire is $B=\frac{\mu_0 I}{2\pi r}=\frac{(4\pi\times10^{-7})(15)}{2\pi(0.02)}=1.5\times10^{-4}$ T. Doubling this (using $r$ instead of $2\pi r$ in the wrong place) gives $3.0\times10^{-4}$ T, halving it gives $7.5\times10^{-5}$ T, and using $r$ in millimetres instead of metres gives $1.5\times10^{-6}$ T.', 'Magnetic effects of currents', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'Two long, straight, parallel wires 5.0 cm apart carry currents of 10 A and 15 A respectively in the same direction. What is the force per unit length between them, and is it attractive or repulsive?', '["$6.0\\times10^{-4}$ N/m, attractive", "$6.0\\times10^{-4}$ N/m, repulsive", "$3.0\\times10^{-4}$ N/m, attractive", "$1.2\\times10^{-3}$ N/m, attractive"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The force per unit length between two parallel current-carrying wires is $\frac{F}{L}=\frac{\mu_0 I_1 I_2}{2\pi d}=\frac{(4\pi\times10^{-7})(10)(15)}{2\pi(0.05)}\approx6.0\times10^{-4}$ N/m. Parallel currents flowing in the same direction attract each other. Halving the force (to $3.0\times10^{-4}$ N/m) comes from a missing factor of 2, and doubling it comes from placing $d$ in the numerator instead of the denominator; currents in the same direction never repel.', 'Magnetic effects of currents', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A single circular loop of wire of radius 10 cm carries a current of 4.0 A. What is the magnetic field at the centre of the loop?', '["$2.51\\times10^{-5}$ T", "$5.03\\times10^{-5}$ T", "$1.26\\times10^{-5}$ T", "$2.51\\times10^{-6}$ T"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The field at the centre of a single circular current loop is $B=\frac{\mu_0 I}{2R}=\frac{(4\pi\times10^{-7})(4.0)}{2(0.1)}\approx2.51\times10^{-5}$ T. Doubling this comes from omitting the factor of 2 in the denominator, halving it from an extra factor of 2, and $2.51\times10^{-6}$ T from mistakenly using the radius in centimetres directly.', 'Magnetic effects of currents', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A long solenoid has 2000 turns per metre and carries a current of 3.0 A. What is the magnetic field inside the solenoid, far from its ends?', '["$7.54\\times10^{-3}$ T", "$3.77\\times10^{-3}$ T", "$1.51\\times10^{-2}$ T", "$7.54\\times10^{-4}$ T"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Deep inside a long solenoid, the field is uniform and given by $B=\mu_0 n I=(4\pi\times10^{-7})(2000)(3.0)\approx7.54\times10^{-3}$ T, where $n$ is turns per metre. Halving this comes from forgetting to include the full turns density, doubling it from an extra factor of 2, and $7.54\times10^{-4}$ T from mistakenly treating $n$ as turns per centimetre.', 'Magnetic effects of currents', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A toroid with 800 turns and a mean radius of 15 cm carries a current of 2.0 A. What is the magnetic field inside the toroid along its mean radius?', '["$2.13\\times10^{-3}$ T", "$1.07\\times10^{-3}$ T", "$4.27\\times10^{-3}$ T", "$2.13\\times10^{-4}$ T"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The field inside a toroid is $B=\frac{\mu_0 N I}{2\pi r}=\frac{(4\pi\times10^{-7})(800)(2.0)}{2\pi(0.15)}\approx2.13\times10^{-3}$ T. Halving this comes from an extra factor of 2 in the denominator, doubling it from omitting a factor of 2, and $2.13\times10^{-4}$ T from using the radius in millimetres by mistake.', 'Magnetic effects of currents', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A straight wire of length 0.40 m carrying a current of 5.0 A is placed perpendicular to a uniform magnetic field of 0.30 T. What is the magnitude of the force on the wire?', '["0.60 N", "0.30 N", "1.20 N", "0.06 N"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The force on a current-carrying wire in a magnetic field is $F=BIL\sin\theta$. With $\theta=90^{\circ}$, $\sin\theta=1$, so $F=(0.30)(5.0)(0.40)=0.60$ N. Halving this (0.30 N) comes from a missing factor, doubling it (1.20 N) from an extra factor of 2, and 0.06 N from misplacing a decimal point in the current.', 'Magnetic effects of currents', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A circular current loop of radius 6.0 cm carrying a current of 5.0 A lies in a plane perpendicular to its axis. What is the magnetic field at a point on the axis, 8.0 cm from the centre of the loop?', '["$1.13\\times10^{-5}$ T", "$2.62\\times10^{-5}$ T", "$5.03\\times10^{-5}$ T", "$1.13\\times10^{-4}$ T"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The field on the axis of a circular loop at distance $x$ from its centre is $B=\frac{\mu_0 I R^2}{2(R^2+x^2)^{3/2}}=\frac{(4\pi\times10^{-7})(5.0)(0.06)^2}{2[(0.06)^2+(0.08)^2]^{3/2}}\approx1.13\times10^{-5}$ T. Using the field-at-centre formula $\mu_0 I/2R$ instead (ignoring the axial distance) gives $2.62\times10^{-5}$ T, a common error that neglects the $(R^2+x^2)^{3/2}$ term.', 'Magnetic effects of currents', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'Two long straight parallel wires carry currents in opposite directions. What is the nature of the magnetic force between them?', '["Repulsive", "Attractive", "Zero, since opposite currents cancel", "It alternates between attractive and repulsive"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Using the right-hand rule, each wire''s magnetic field at the location of the other, combined with the force law $\vec{F}=I\vec{L}\times\vec{B}$, shows that parallel currents flowing in opposite directions push the wires apart. This is the opposite of the attraction seen when the currents flow in the same direction.', 'Magnetic effects of currents', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'Which statement correctly describes the direction of the magnetic field lines around a long straight current-carrying wire?', '["They form concentric circles around the wire, with direction given by the right-hand grip rule", "They point radially outward from the wire in straight lines", "They run parallel to the wire in the direction of the current", "They form circles that all pass through a single point on the wire"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Ampere''s law and the Biot-Savart law both show that the magnetic field around a long straight current-carrying wire forms closed concentric circles centred on the wire, lying in planes perpendicular to it. The sense of circulation is given by the right-hand grip rule: if the thumb points along the current, the curled fingers show the field direction.', 'Magnetic effects of currents', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A long straight current-carrying wire is placed in a region where a compass needle initially points due north. When the wire is placed directly above the compass, running north-south, and current flows in the wire, what determines whether the needle deflects east or west?', '["The direction of the current in the wire, via the right-hand rule", "Only the magnitude of the current, not its direction", "The colour of the wire''s insulation", "The needle will not deflect regardless of current direction"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The wire''s current produces a circular magnetic field around it (right-hand grip rule), and at the compass location below the wire this field has a definite east or west component depending on whether the current flows north or south. The compass needle aligns with the combined field of the Earth and the wire, so reversing the current direction reverses the deflection.', 'Magnetic effects of currents', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A flat circular coil of 200 turns and area $0.05\ \text{m}^2$ is placed with its plane perpendicular to a magnetic field that increases uniformly from 0.10 T to 0.30 T in 0.40 s. What is the magnitude of the average EMF induced in the coil?', '["5.0 V", "2.5 V", "10.0 V", "0.5 V"]'::jsonb, 0, 'By Faraday''s law, $\varepsilon = N\frac{\Delta\Phi}{\Delta t} = N A\frac{\Delta B}{\Delta t} = (200)(0.05)\frac{(0.30-0.10)}{0.40} = 5.0$ V. Halving this (2.5 V) comes from forgetting to multiply by N, doubling it (10.0 V) from an extra factor of 2, and 0.5 V from using the area in $\text{cm}^2$ instead of $\text{m}^2$.', 'Faraday''s law of induction', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A straight conducting rod of length 0.60 m moves at a constant velocity of 4.0 m/s perpendicular to a uniform magnetic field of 0.25 T, with its length also perpendicular to its velocity. What EMF is induced between the ends of the rod?', '["0.60 V", "0.15 V", "1.2 V", "0.06 V"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The motional EMF is $\varepsilon = BLv = (0.25)(0.60)(4.0) = 0.60$ V. Using $BL/v$ instead of $BLv$ gives 0.15 V, doubling the correct product gives 1.2 V, and 0.06 V results from a misplaced decimal point in the length.', 'Faraday''s law of induction', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A rectangular coil of 150 turns, area $0.02\ \text{m}^2$, rotates at a constant angular velocity of 50 rad/s in a uniform magnetic field of 0.40 T, with the rotation axis perpendicular to the field. What is the peak EMF generated?', '["60 V", "30 V", "120 V", "1.5 V"]'::jsonb, 0, 'For a coil rotating in a uniform field, the peak EMF is $\varepsilon_0 = NBA\omega = (150)(0.40)(0.02)(50) = 60$ V. Halving this (30 V) comes from forgetting a factor, doubling it (120 V) from an extra factor of 2, and 1.5 V from omitting $\omega$ entirely.', 'Faraday''s law of induction', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A bar magnet is pushed, north pole first, toward a stationary closed conducting loop. According to Lenz''s law, what is the direction of the induced current in the loop, as viewed from the side the magnet approaches?', '["Such that the loop''s near face becomes a north pole, opposing the magnet''s approach", "Such that the loop''s near face becomes a south pole, attracting the magnet", "There is no induced current since the magnet''s field is constant in magnitude", "The direction alternates rapidly regardless of the magnet''s motion"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Lenz''s law states that the induced current opposes the change in flux that produces it. As the north pole approaches, the flux through the loop (in that direction) increases, so the induced current flows in the sense that makes the near face of the loop a north pole, repelling the approaching magnet and opposing its motion, consistent with conservation of energy.', 'Faraday''s law of induction', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A closed conducting loop of resistance $4.0\ \Omega$ encloses an area of $0.10\ \text{m}^2$. If the magnetic field through the loop changes uniformly at a rate of 2.0 T/s, what current flows in the loop?', '["0.05 A", "0.20 A", "0.025 A", "0.80 A"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The induced EMF is $\varepsilon = A\frac{dB}{dt} = (0.10)(2.0) = 0.20$ V, and by Ohm''s law $I = \varepsilon/R = 0.20/4.0 = 0.05$ A. Skipping the division by resistance gives 0.20 A, multiplying by R instead of dividing gives 0.80 A, and 0.025 A comes from halving the correct EMF before dividing.', 'Faraday''s law of induction', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'Solid metal blocks moving through a magnetic field, or exposed to a changing magnetic field, develop swirling induced currents within their bulk. What are these currents called, and what is one common practical effect of them?', '["Eddy currents; they dissipate energy as heat, which is exploited in induction heating and braking", "Displacement currents; they store energy without loss, used in capacitors", "Hall currents; they only occur in semiconductors, not metals", "Leakage currents; they indicate a fault and should always be eliminated"]'::jsonb, 0, 'When a bulk conductor experiences a changing magnetic flux, Faraday''s law induces circulating currents within its volume, known as eddy currents. Because the metal has resistance, these currents dissipate electrical energy as heat, an effect deliberately used in induction heating and electromagnetic braking, though it is often an unwanted power loss in transformer cores.', 'Faraday''s law of induction', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'For an induced EMF to appear in a closed loop according to Faraday''s law, what condition must be met?', '["The magnetic flux linked with the loop must be changing with time", "The loop must be made of a ferromagnetic material", "The magnetic field must be non-uniform in space at every instant", "The loop must be moving at constant velocity through a uniform field"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Faraday''s law states that an EMF is induced whenever the magnetic flux through a closed loop changes with time, $\varepsilon = -\frac{d\Phi}{dt}$. This change can arise from a varying field strength, a changing loop area, or a changing angle between the loop and the field; the loop material and the specific way the flux changes are not what matters, only that it does change.', 'Faraday''s law of induction', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A square coil of side 0.20 m and 100 turns lies with its plane perpendicular to a magnetic field of 0.50 T. The coil is pulled out of the field region in 0.25 s, reducing the field through it to zero. What is the magnitude of the average induced EMF?', '["8.0 V", "4.0 V", "16.0 V", "0.8 V"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The area of the coil is $A = (0.20)^2 = 0.04\ \text{m}^2$, and the flux change is $\Delta\Phi = A\Delta B = (0.04)(0.50) = 0.02$ Wb. The average EMF is $\varepsilon = N\frac{\Delta\Phi}{\Delta t} = (100)\frac{0.02}{0.25} = 8.0$ V. Halving this (4.0 V) comes from forgetting the number of turns, doubling it (16.0 V) from an extra factor, and 0.8 V from mistakenly using the side length instead of the area.', 'Faraday''s law of induction', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A solenoid has 500 turns, a cross-sectional area of $3.0\times10^{-4}\ \text{m}^2$, and a length of 0.25 m. What is its self-inductance?', '["$3.77\\times10^{-4}$ H", "$1.88\\times10^{-4}$ H", "$7.54\\times10^{-4}$ H", "$3.77\\times10^{-2}$ H"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The self-inductance of a long solenoid is $L=\frac{\mu_0 N^2 A}{l}=\frac{(4\pi\times10^{-7})(500)^2(3.0\times10^{-4})}{0.25}\approx3.77\times10^{-4}$ H. Halving this comes from using $N$ instead of $N^2$, doubling it from an extra factor of 2, and $3.77\times10^{-2}$ H from mistakenly using the length in centimetres.', 'Inductance and energy storage in magnetic fields', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'An inductor of inductance 0.20 H carries a steady current of 4.0 A. How much energy is stored in its magnetic field?', '["1.6 J", "0.8 J", "3.2 J", "0.16 J"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The energy stored in an inductor''s magnetic field is $U=\frac{1}{2}LI^2=\frac{1}{2}(0.20)(4.0)^2=1.6$ J. Forgetting the factor of $\frac{1}{2}$ gives 3.2 J, using $I$ instead of $I^2$ gives 0.8 J, and 0.16 J results from mistakenly using $I=0.4$ A.', 'Inductance and energy storage in magnetic fields', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'What is the energy density of the magnetic field in a region where the magnetic flux density is 0.50 T?', '["$9.95\\times10^4\\ \\text{J/m}^3$", "$1.99\\times10^5\\ \\text{J/m}^3$", "$4.97\\times10^4\\ \\text{J/m}^3$", "$1.25\\ \\text{J/m}^3$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The energy density stored in a magnetic field is $u=\frac{B^2}{2\mu_0}=\frac{(0.50)^2}{2(4\pi\times10^{-7})}\approx9.95\times10^4\ \text{J/m}^3$. Forgetting the factor of 2 in the denominator gives $1.99\times10^5\ \text{J/m}^3$, using an extra factor of 2 gives $4.97\times10^4\ \text{J/m}^3$, and using $B$ instead of $B^2$ (with $\mu_0$ left undivided) gives an answer far too small.', 'Inductance and energy storage in magnetic fields', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'In a series circuit containing a resistor of $15\ \Omega$ and an inductor of 0.60 H, connected to a battery, what is the time constant of the circuit, and what fraction of the final steady current has been reached after one time constant has elapsed?', '["0.040 s; about 63%", "0.040 s; about 37%", "9.0 s; about 63%", "0.020 s; about 50%"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The time constant of an LR circuit is $\tau=L/R=0.60/15=0.040$ s. The current grows as $I=I_0(1-e^{-t/\tau})$, so after one time constant ($t=\tau$), the fraction reached is $1-e^{-1}\approx0.632$, i.e. about 63%. Using $e^{-1}\approx0.37$ directly as the fraction reached (rather than $1-e^{-1}$) is a common mix-up between the growing and decaying exponential terms.', 'Inductance and energy storage in magnetic fields', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'Two coils are placed near each other so that a changing current in coil 1 induces an EMF in coil 2. If the mutual inductance between them is 0.05 H and the current in coil 1 changes at a rate of 20 A/s, what is the magnitude of the EMF induced in coil 2?', '["1.0 V", "0.5 V", "2.0 V", "0.05 V"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The mutually induced EMF is given by $\varepsilon_2 = M\frac{dI_1}{dt} = (0.05)(20) = 1.0$ V. Halving this (0.5 V) comes from a missing factor, doubling it (2.0 V) from an extra factor of 2, and 0.05 V results from mistakenly ignoring the rate of change and using $M$ alone.', 'Inductance and energy storage in magnetic fields', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A long solenoid produces a magnetic field $B_0$ inside it when empty. When its core is filled with a certain substance, the field becomes $B$. The ratio $B/B_0$, which is characteristic of that substance, is called its', '["Relative permeability", "Magnetic susceptibility", "Magnetization", "Electric permittivity"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The ratio $K_m = B/B_0$ is defined as the relative permeability of the substance filling the solenoid; the field inside becomes $B = K_m B_0$.', 'Magnetic properties of matter', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A substance has relative permeability $K_m$. In terms of $K_m$, its magnetic susceptibility $\chi_m$ is defined as', '["$\\chi_m = K_m - 1$", "$\\chi_m = K_m + 1$", "$\\chi_m = 1/K_m$", "$\\chi_m = K_m^2 - 1$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Magnetic susceptibility is defined as $\chi_m = K_m - 1 = B/B_0 - 1$. A positive $\chi_m$ means $K_m > 1$ (paramagnetic or ferromagnetic behaviour), while a negative $\chi_m$ means $K_m < 1$ (diamagnetic behaviour).', 'Magnetic properties of matter', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'At room temperature, the magnetic susceptibilities of four substances are measured as: aluminium $+2.10\times10^{-5}$, mercury $-2.80\times10^{-5}$, ferric chloride $+306\times10^{-5}$, and diamond $-2.20\times10^{-5}$. Which pair of these substances is diamagnetic?', '["Mercury and diamond", "Aluminium and ferric chloride", "Aluminium and mercury", "Ferric chloride and diamond"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A negative magnetic susceptibility indicates a diamagnetic substance. Mercury ($-2.80\times10^{-5}$) and diamond ($-2.20\times10^{-5}$) both have negative susceptibilities, so they are diamagnetic. Aluminium and ferric chloride have positive susceptibilities and are paramagnetic.', 'Magnetic properties of matter', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A flat loop of wire of area $A$ carries a steady current $I$. The magnitude of the magnetic (dipole) moment set up by this current loop is', '["$\\mu = IA$", "$\\mu = I/A$", "$\\mu = IA^2$", "$\\mu = I^2A$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A current loop of area $A$ carrying current $I$ sets up a magnetic dipole moment of magnitude $\mu = IA$, directed normal to the plane of the loop. The tiny current loops formed by orbiting and spinning electrons in atoms are the microscopic source of a material''s magnetic moment.', 'Magnetic properties of matter', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'In a ferromagnetic material below its critical temperature, neighbouring atomic magnetic moments align spontaneously within small regions even without any applied field. This spontaneous alignment is primarily due to', '["A quantum-mechanical exchange coupling force between neighbouring atoms", "The electrostatic repulsion between neighbouring electrons", "The gravitational attraction between neighbouring nuclei", "An externally applied electric field"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The spontaneous alignment of atomic magnetic moments within a domain is due to an exchange coupling force between adjacent atoms, whose origin lies in quantum mechanics rather than in classical magnetic or electrostatic interactions.', 'Magnetic properties of matter', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A small region within a ferromagnetic crystal in which all the atomic magnetic moments are aligned in the same direction, even though the sample as a whole may show no overall magnetization, is called a', '["Magnetic domain", "Unit cell", "Magnetic dipole", "Coercive region"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Such a region of aligned atomic magnetic moments is called a domain. In an unmagnetized ferromagnetic sample the domains themselves point in random directions, giving zero net magnetization, but applying an external field grows and rotates the favourably oriented domains.', 'Magnetic properties of matter', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A paramagnetic sample has a Curie constant of $1.5\text{ K}$ and is placed in a magnetic field of $0.6\text{ T}$ at a temperature of $10\ ^\circ\text{C}$. Using Curie''s law, what is the resulting magnetization of the sample?', '["$3.18\\times10^{-3}\\ \\text{A m}^{-1}$", "$1.5\\times10^{-2}\\ \\text{A m}^{-1}$", "$9.0\\times10^{-1}\\ \\text{A m}^{-1}$", "$4.72\\times10^{-2}\\ \\text{A m}^{-1}$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Curie''s law gives $M = CB/T$. Converting the temperature to kelvin, $T = 10 + 273.15 = 283.15\text{ K}$, so $M = (1.5)(0.6)/283.15 \approx 3.18\times10^{-3}\ \text{A m}^{-1}$.', 'Magnetic properties of matter', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'The relative permeability of the iron filling a solenoid''s core is $5500$. Taking the permeability of free space as $\mu_0 = 1.26\times10^{-6}\ \text{H m}^{-1}$, what is the permeability of the iron?', '["$6.93\\times10^{-3}\\ \\text{H m}^{-1}$", "$5500\\ \\text{H m}^{-1}$", "$1.26\\times10^{-6}\\ \\text{H m}^{-1}$", "$4.37\\times10^{9}\\ \\text{H m}^{-1}$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The permeability of the filled core is $\mu = K_m \mu_0 = 5500 \times 1.26\times10^{-6} \approx 6.93\times10^{-3}\ \text{H m}^{-1}$.', 'Magnetic properties of matter', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A long air-core solenoid with a total of $4000$ turns carries a steady current of $2\text{ A}$ and produces a magnetic field of $0.02\text{ T}$ inside it. What is the length of the solenoid?', '["$0.503\\text{ m}$", "$0.251\\text{ m}$", "$1.005\\text{ m}$", "$2.011\\text{ m}$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'For an air-core solenoid, $B_0 = \mu_0 n I$ where $n = N/L$. So $L = \mu_0 N I / B_0 = (4\pi\times10^{-7})(4000)(2)/0.02 \approx 0.503\text{ m}$.', 'Magnetic properties of matter', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A toroid has $1000$ turns wound uniformly around a mean radius of $25\text{ cm}$. Ignoring any core material, what magnetic field is produced inside the toroid when it carries a current of $1250\text{ A}$?', '["$1.0\\text{ T}$", "$0.5\\text{ T}$", "$2.0\\text{ T}$", "$6.3\\text{ T}$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'For a toroid, $B = \mu_0 N I/(2\pi r) = (4\pi\times10^{-7})(1000)(1250)/(2\pi \times 0.25) = 1.0\text{ T}$.', 'Magnetic properties of matter', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'The magnetic susceptibility of iron ammonium alum is measured as $7.54\times10^{-3}$. What is its relative permeability?', '["$1.00754$", "$7.54\\times10^{-3}$", "$0.99246$", "$132.6$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Since $\chi_m = K_m - 1$, the relative permeability is $K_m = 1 + \chi_m = 1 + 7.54\times10^{-3} = 1.00754$. Because $K_m$ is slightly greater than unity, the material is paramagnetic.', 'Magnetic properties of matter', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'The relative permeability of cobalt is $240$, while that of copper is $-0.96$. What is the ratio of the magnetic susceptibility of cobalt to that of copper?', '["$-121.9$", "$-250.0$", "$293.0$", "$-0.242$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Susceptibility is $\chi_m = K_m - 1$. For cobalt, $\chi_m = 240 - 1 = 239$; for copper, $\chi_m = -0.96 - 1 = -1.96$. The ratio is $239/(-1.96) \approx -121.9$.', 'Magnetic properties of matter', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'The relative permeabilities of four materials are measured as $0.9999832$, $1.00754$, $1.00385$ and $0.9999957$. How many of these four materials are diamagnetic?', '["2", "1", "3", "4"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A material is diamagnetic when its relative permeability is less than unity. Here $0.9999832$ and $0.9999957$ are both below $1$, so exactly two of the four materials are diamagnetic; the other two ($1.00754$ and $1.00385$) are paramagnetic.', 'Magnetic properties of matter', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'Which of the following metals is essentially unaffected by an ordinary permanent magnet, showing negligible attraction towards it?', '["Tin", "Cobalt", "Nickel", "Iron"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Cobalt, nickel and iron are ferromagnetic and are strongly attracted to a magnet. Tin is not ferromagnetic and shows no significant attraction to an ordinary magnet.', 'Magnetic properties of matter', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'Which of the following statements about magnetizing ferromagnetic objects is correct?', '["A magnetized steel object retains its magnetism much more readily than a magnetized soft-iron object", "A magnetized soft-iron object retains its magnetism permanently once the magnetizing field is removed", "Stroking an object back and forth in alternating directions is the most effective way to magnetize it", "Steel is easier to magnetize than soft iron"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Steel has a much larger coercivity than soft iron, so once magnetized it holds its magnetism well and is not easily demagnetized by shaking or stray fields, making it suitable for permanent magnets. Soft iron, with its small coercivity, loses its magnetism readily once the field is removed. Stroking an object consistently in one direction (not back and forth) is the effective way to magnetize it, and steel is in fact harder, not easier, to magnetize than soft iron.', 'Magnetic properties of matter', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A material is needed for a transformer core, where it will be repeatedly magnetized and demagnetized at mains frequency. To keep the energy wasted as heat in each cycle as small as possible, the core should be made from a material with', '["Low coercivity and a small hysteresis loop area", "High coercivity and a large hysteresis loop area", "Zero relative permeability", "A negative magnetic susceptibility"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The energy dissipated as heat per magnetization cycle is proportional to the area enclosed by the hysteresis loop. A soft ferromagnetic material with low coercivity has a narrow loop and hence low hysteresis loss, making it suitable for transformer cores that are cycled continuously; high-coercivity materials are instead better suited to permanent magnets.', 'Magnetic properties of matter', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'After a ferromagnetic sample is magnetized to saturation and the external field is then reduced to zero, a reverse field must be applied to bring its magnetic field back down to zero. The magnitude of this reverse field is called the sample''s', '["Coercive force", "Remanence", "Saturation field", "Curie temperature"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The reverse field strength needed to reduce the residual (remanent) field to zero is called the coercive force. It reflects how strongly the domains resist being disaligned once magnetized, which is why materials with high coercivity are hard to demagnetize.', 'Magnetic properties of matter', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'As a ferromagnetic material is heated, thermal agitation increasingly disrupts the alignment of neighbouring atomic dipoles within its domains. Above a certain critical temperature, all ferromagnetic ordering is lost and the material behaves as a paramagnet instead. This critical temperature is called the', '["Curie temperature", "Coercive temperature", "Saturation temperature", "Absolute temperature"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The temperature above which a ferromagnetic material loses its spontaneous domain alignment and becomes paramagnetic is called the Curie temperature; above it, thermal energy overwhelms the exchange coupling that otherwise aligns neighbouring atomic dipoles.', 'Magnetic properties of matter', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'The magnetic dipole moment per unit volume of a magnetized sample is called its', '["Magnetization", "Magnetic susceptibility", "Permeability", "Magnetic flux density"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Magnetization $\vec{M}$ is defined as the magnetic dipole moment per unit volume of a sample, $\vec{M} = \vec{\mu}/V$, where $\vec{\mu}$ is the total magnetic dipole moment of the sample.', 'Magnetic properties of matter', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'An alternating voltage has a peak value of 340 V. What is its root-mean-square (rms) value?', '["240 V", "480 V", "170 V", "340 V"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The rms value of a sinusoidal voltage is $V_{rms}=\frac{V_0}{\sqrt2}=\frac{340}{\sqrt2}\approx240$ V. Multiplying instead of dividing by $\sqrt2$ gives 480 V, halving the peak directly gives 170 V, and taking rms equal to the peak ignores the averaging over a cycle entirely.', 'Alternating current and AC circuits', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A $25\ \mu\text{F}$ capacitor is connected to a 60 Hz alternating supply. What is its reactance?', '["106 $\\Omega$", "53 $\\Omega$", "212 $\\Omega$", "9.4 $\\times10^{-3}\\ \\Omega$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The capacitive reactance is $X_C=\frac{1}{2\pi fC}=\frac{1}{2\pi(60)(25\times10^{-6})}\approx106\ \Omega$. Doubling this (an extra factor of 2 in the denominator''s omission) gives 212 $\Omega$, halving it gives 53 $\Omega$, and inverting the formula (using $2\pi fC$ directly instead of its reciprocal) gives a value in siemens rather than ohms, $9.4\times10^{-3}$.', 'Alternating current and AC circuits', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'An inductor of inductance 0.15 H is connected to a 60 Hz alternating supply. What is its reactance?', '["56.5 $\\Omega$", "28.3 $\\Omega$", "113 $\\Omega$", "9.0 $\\Omega$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The inductive reactance is $X_L=2\pi fL=2\pi(60)(0.15)\approx56.5\ \Omega$. Halving this (28.3 $\Omega$) comes from a missing factor of 2, doubling it (113 $\Omega$) from an extra factor of 2, and 9.0 $\Omega$ from omitting the $2\pi$ factor and using $fL$ alone.', 'Alternating current and AC circuits', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A resistor of $40\ \Omega$ is connected in series with a $20\ \mu\text{F}$ capacitor across a 50 Hz alternating supply. What is the impedance of the circuit?', '["164 $\\Omega$", "199 $\\Omega$", "159 $\\Omega$", "40 $\\Omega$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'First, $X_C=\frac{1}{2\pi fC}=\frac{1}{2\pi(50)(20\times10^{-6})}\approx159\ \Omega$. Since R and C are in series, the impedance is $Z=\sqrt{R^2+X_C^2}=\sqrt{40^2+159^2}\approx164\ \Omega$. Simply adding R and $X_C$ instead of combining them as a vector sum gives 199 $\Omega$, using $X_C$ alone ignores the resistor, and using R alone ignores the capacitor.', 'Alternating current and AC circuits', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A resistor of $30\ \Omega$ is connected in series with an inductor of inductance 0.10 H across a 50 Hz alternating supply. What is the impedance of the circuit?', '["43.4 $\\Omega$", "61.4 $\\Omega$", "31.4 $\\Omega$", "30 $\\Omega$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'First, $X_L=2\pi fL=2\pi(50)(0.10)\approx31.4\ \Omega$. Since R and L are in series, $Z=\sqrt{R^2+X_L^2}=\sqrt{30^2+31.4^2}\approx43.4\ \Omega$. Adding R and $X_L$ directly instead of combining as a vector sum gives 61.4 $\Omega$, and using $X_L$ or R alone ignores the other component.', 'Alternating current and AC circuits', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A series circuit contains an inductor of 0.5 H and a capacitor of $20\ \mu\text{F}$. At what frequency does the circuit resonate?', '["50.3 Hz", "25.2 Hz", "100.6 Hz", "6.37 Hz"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The resonant frequency of a series LC (or RLC) circuit is $f_0=\frac{1}{2\pi\sqrt{LC}}=\frac{1}{2\pi\sqrt{(0.5)(20\times10^{-6})}}\approx50.3$ Hz. Halving this (25.2 Hz) comes from an extra factor of 2 under the square root, doubling it (100.6 Hz) from a missing factor, and 6.37 Hz from omitting the $2\pi$ factor.', 'Alternating current and AC circuits', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A series RLC circuit has $R=25\ \Omega$, $L=0.20$ H, and $C=15\ \mu\text{F}$, connected to a 60 Hz supply. What is the impedance of the circuit?', '["104 $\\Omega$", "252 $\\Omega$", "101 $\\Omega$", "76.4 $\\Omega$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'First, $X_L=2\pi fL=2\pi(60)(0.20)\approx75.4\ \Omega$ and $X_C=\frac{1}{2\pi fC}=\frac{1}{2\pi(60)(15\times10^{-6})}\approx176.8\ \Omega$. The impedance is $Z=\sqrt{R^2+(X_L-X_C)^2}=\sqrt{25^2+(75.4-176.8)^2}\approx104\ \Omega$. Adding $X_L$ and $X_C$ instead of subtracting (as if they reinforce rather than oppose) gives 252 $\Omega$, and 101 $\Omega$ or 76.4 $\Omega$ come from a slightly wrong value of $X_L-X_C$.', 'Alternating current and AC circuits', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'For the RLC circuit above ($R=25\ \Omega$, $X_L\approx75.4\ \Omega$, $X_C\approx176.8\ \Omega$, $Z\approx104\ \Omega$) connected to a 120 V rms supply, what is the average power dissipated in the circuit?', '["33.0 W", "138 W", "8.25 W", "576 W"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The rms current is $I_{rms}=\frac{V_{rms}}{Z}=\frac{120}{104}\approx1.15$ A. Since only the resistor dissipates power on average, $P=I_{rms}^2 R=(1.15)^2(25)\approx33.0$ W. Using $P=\frac{V_{rms}^2}{Z}$ instead of $I_{rms}^2R$ (mixing up impedance with resistance) gives 138 W, forgetting to square the current gives 8.25 W, and using $\frac{V_{rms}^2}{R}$ (ignoring the impedance in finding current) gives 576 W.', 'Alternating current and AC circuits', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A $40\ \mu\text{F}$ capacitor is connected across a 60 Hz alternating supply with an rms voltage of 24 V. What is the peak current flowing in the wires?', '["0.512 A", "0.362 A", "0.256 A", "1.024 A"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The peak voltage is $V_0=V_{rms}\sqrt2=24\sqrt2\approx33.9$ V. The peak current is $I_0=V_0(2\pi fC)=(33.9)(2\pi)(60)(40\times10^{-6})\approx0.512$ A. Using the rms voltage instead of the peak voltage gives 0.362 A, halving the correct value gives 0.256 A, and doubling it gives 1.024 A.', 'Alternating current and AC circuits', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'In a series RC circuit connected to an alternating supply, the current leads the applied voltage. Which of the following correctly explains why?', '["The capacitor''s voltage lags the current by $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ radians, so the resultant voltage across R and C lags behind the current", "The resistor''s voltage lags the current by $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ radians", "The current and voltage are always exactly in phase in any AC circuit", "The capacitor forces the current to lag the voltage by $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ radians"]'::jsonb, 0, 'In a resistor, the voltage $V_R=IR$ is always in phase with the current. In a capacitor, the voltage $V_C=IX_C$ lags the current by $\frac{\pi}{2}$ radians because the current must flow first to build up charge before the voltage across the capacitor rises. Combining these phasors, the resultant applied voltage across the series RC combination lags the current, which is equivalent to saying the current leads the voltage.', 'Alternating current and AC circuits', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'In a series RLC circuit driven at its resonant frequency, which of the following is true?', '["The impedance is at its minimum and equals the resistance R", "The impedance is at its maximum and equals R", "The inductive and capacitive reactances are both zero", "The current in the circuit is at its minimum value"]'::jsonb, 0, 'At resonance, $X_L=X_C$, so the reactive part of the impedance $Z=\sqrt{R^2+(X_L-X_C)^2}$ vanishes and the impedance reduces to its minimum possible value, $Z=R$. Since the current is $I=\frac{V}{Z}$, this minimum impedance corresponds to the maximum current, not the minimum, and the individual reactances $X_L$ and $X_C$ are equal and typically non-zero, not zero.', 'Alternating current and AC circuits', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A 100 W incandescent lamp is rated for 240 V rms mains but is instead connected to a 120 V rms alternating supply of the same frequency. Assuming the lamp''s resistance stays constant, what current flows through it?', '["0.208 A", "0.417 A", "0.104 A", "0.833 A"]'::jsonb, 0, 'At the rated voltage, the lamp''s resistance is $R=\frac{V^2}{P}=\frac{240^2}{100}=576\ \Omega$. At the new supply voltage, the current is $I=\frac{V}{R}=\frac{120}{576}\approx0.208$ A. Using the rated voltage instead of 120 V for the current calculation gives 0.417 A, and the other distractors come from mixing up the ratio of voltages incorrectly (e.g. assuming current scales with the square or inverse of voltage).', 'Alternating current and AC circuits', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A capacitor and a pure resistor are connected in series across a 100 V rms alternating supply. If the rms voltage across the capacitor is measured to be 80 V, what is the rms voltage across the resistor?', '["60 V", "20 V", "180 V", "64 V"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Because the resistor''s voltage is in phase with the current while the capacitor''s voltage lags it by $\frac{\pi}{2}$ radians, the two voltages add as perpendicular phasors: $V^2=V_R^2+V_C^2$. So $V_R=\sqrt{V^2-V_C^2}=\sqrt{100^2-80^2}=\sqrt{3600}=60$ V. Simply subtracting the voltages arithmetically ($100-80=20$ V) ignores their phase difference, and adding them (180 V) or multiplying $V_C$ by 0.8 (64 V) are also inconsistent with the phasor relationship.', 'Alternating current and AC circuits', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'An inductor with negligible resistance is connected to a 20 Hz alternating supply that provides an rms voltage of 50 V. What inductance is required to keep the instantaneous current in the circuit always below 80 mA?', '["At least 7.04 H", "At least 3.98 H", "At least 14.08 H", "At most 7.04 H"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The peak current must not exceed 80 mA, and $I_0=\frac{V_0}{X_L}=\frac{V_0}{2\pi fL}$, with $V_0=V_{rms}\sqrt2\approx70.7$ V. Setting $I_0=0.080$ A and solving for L: $L=\frac{V_0}{2\pi f I_0}=\frac{70.7}{2\pi(20)(0.080)}\approx7.04$ H, and since reactance increases with L, the inductance must be at least this value to keep the current at or below the limit. Using $V_{rms}$ instead of $V_0$ in the numerator gives 3.98 H, and doubling the correct value gives 14.08 H; choosing ''at most'' inverts the required inequality, since a larger inductance gives a smaller current.', 'Alternating current and AC circuits', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'Which of Maxwell''s equations expresses the experimental fact that isolated magnetic poles (monopoles) have never been observed?', '["Gauss''s law for magnetism, $\\oint B_n\\,dA = 0$", "Gauss''s law for electricity, $\\oint E_n\\,dA = Q/\\varepsilon_0$", "Faraday''s law of induction", "The Ampere-Maxwell law"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Gauss''s law for magnetism states that the net magnetic flux through any closed surface is always zero, meaning magnetic field lines never diverge from or converge on a point the way electric field lines do from a charge. This directly reflects the absence of magnetic monopoles: every field line that enters a closed surface must also leave it.', 'Maxwell''s equations', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'What key modification did Maxwell make to Ampere''s law to produce the Ampere-Maxwell law, and what did this modification predict?', '["He added a term for the rate of change of electric flux, predicting that a changing electric field can generate a magnetic field even without a conduction current", "He removed the current term entirely, predicting that circuits with no current can still exist", "He added a term for the rate of change of magnetic flux, duplicating Faraday''s law", "He introduced the permittivity of free space for the first time in physics"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Maxwell recognised that Ampere''s original law, $\oint B\cdot dl = \mu_0 I$, was incomplete for circuits with a changing electric field (such as between charging capacitor plates). He added the displacement current term $\mu_0\varepsilon_0\frac{d}{dt}\int E_n\,dA$, showing that a time-varying electric field itself acts as a source of magnetic field, symmetric with Faraday''s law in which a changing magnetic field produces an electric field.', 'Maxwell''s equations', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'According to Maxwell''s equations, the speed of electromagnetic waves in free space is related to the permeability $\mu_0$ and permittivity $\varepsilon_0$ of free space by which expression?', '["$c = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\varepsilon_0}}$", "$c = \\mu_0\\varepsilon_0$", "$c = \\sqrt{\\mu_0\\varepsilon_0}$", "$c = \\dfrac{\\mu_0}{\\varepsilon_0}$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Combining Maxwell''s equations to derive a wave equation for the electric and magnetic fields in free space shows that electromagnetic waves travel at $c=\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{\mu_0\varepsilon_0}}$. Substituting $\mu_0=4\pi\times10^{-7}$ H/m and $\varepsilon_0=8.854\times10^{-12}$ F/m gives $c\approx3\times10^8$ m/s, matching the measured speed of light and confirming that light itself is an electromagnetic wave.', 'Maxwell''s equations', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A typical AM radio station broadcasts at a frequency of 1000 kHz. What is the corresponding wavelength of this radio wave in free space?', '["300 m", "150 m", "600 m", "3.0 m"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Using $c=f\lambda$, the wavelength is $\lambda = c/f = \dfrac{3\times10^8}{1\times10^6} = 300$ m. Halving this (150 m) comes from a factor-of-2 slip, doubling it (600 m) from the reciprocal slip, and 3.0 m results from mistakenly treating the frequency as 100 MHz instead of 1000 kHz.', 'Maxwell''s equations', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A typical FM radio station broadcasts at a frequency of 100 MHz. What is the corresponding wavelength of this radio wave in free space?', '["3.0 m", "30 m", "1.5 m", "300 m"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Using $c=f\lambda$, the wavelength is $\lambda = c/f = \dfrac{3\times10^8}{1\times10^8} = 3.0$ m. Multiplying instead of dividing gives 30 m or 300 m depending on the power-of-ten error made, and halving the correct answer gives 1.5 m.', 'Maxwell''s equations', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A radar system emits microwaves of wavelength 3.0 cm. What is the frequency of this radiation?', '["$1.0\\times10^{10}$ Hz", "$5.0\\times10^{9}$ Hz", "$2.0\\times10^{10}$ Hz", "$1.0\\times10^{8}$ Hz"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Using $c=f\lambda$, the frequency is $f = c/\lambda = \dfrac{3\times10^8}{3.0\times10^{-2}} = 1.0\times10^{10}$ Hz. Halving this ($5.0\times10^9$ Hz) or doubling it ($2.0\times10^{10}$ Hz) come from factor-of-2 slips, and $1.0\times10^8$ Hz results from mistakenly using the wavelength in metres as if it were 3.0 m.', 'Maxwell''s equations', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'An X-ray photon has a wavelength of 0.10 nm. What is the frequency of this radiation?', '["$3.0\\times10^{18}$ Hz", "$1.5\\times10^{18}$ Hz", "$6.0\\times10^{18}$ Hz", "$3.0\\times10^{16}$ Hz"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Using $c=f\lambda$, the frequency is $f=c/\lambda=\dfrac{3\times10^8}{0.10\times10^{-9}}=3.0\times10^{18}$ Hz. Halving this ($1.5\times10^{18}$ Hz) or doubling it ($6.0\times10^{18}$ Hz) come from arithmetic slips, and $3.0\times10^{16}$ Hz results from mistakenly using a wavelength of 10 nm instead of 0.10 nm.', 'Maxwell''s equations', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'Arranging gamma rays, visible light, radio waves, and X-rays in order of increasing wavelength (shortest to longest), which sequence is correct?', '["Gamma rays, X-rays, visible light, radio waves", "Radio waves, visible light, X-rays, gamma rays", "Visible light, gamma rays, X-rays, radio waves", "X-rays, gamma rays, radio waves, visible light"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Across the electromagnetic spectrum, wavelength and frequency are inversely related through $c=f\lambda$. Gamma rays have the shortest wavelengths and highest frequencies and energies, followed by X-rays, then ultraviolet, visible light, infrared, microwaves, and finally radio waves with the longest wavelengths and lowest frequencies.', 'Maxwell''s equations', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'According to Maxwell''s equations, an electromagnetic wave propagating through free space consists of oscillating electric and magnetic fields with which orientation?', '["Both fields are perpendicular to each other and to the direction of wave propagation", "Both fields are parallel to the direction of propagation", "The electric field is parallel to the propagation direction while the magnetic field is perpendicular to it", "The two fields oscillate independently with no fixed relative orientation"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Maxwell''s equations, applied to a plane wave solution in free space, show that electromagnetic waves are transverse: the oscillating electric field $\vec E$ and magnetic field $\vec B$ are mutually perpendicular, and both are perpendicular to the direction in which the wave travels, with $\vec E \times \vec B$ pointing along the propagation direction.', 'Maxwell''s equations', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'In the Geiger-Marsden alpha-particle scattering experiment, a thin gold foil was bombarded with alpha particles and most passed through with little or no deflection, while a small fraction were deflected through very large angles, some almost straight back. What did the large-angle deflections reveal about the structure of the atom?', '["The atom''s positive charge and most of its mass are concentrated in a tiny, dense central nucleus", "The atom''s positive charge is spread uniformly throughout its volume", "Atoms contain no internal structure and behave as solid, uniform spheres", "Electrons are massive enough to deflect alpha particles through large angles"]'::jsonb, 0, 'If positive charge were spread thinly throughout the atom''s volume (as in the earlier plum-pudding model), the repulsive force on a passing alpha particle would always be weak, and large deflections would essentially never occur. The observed occasional large-angle and near-180-degree scattering could only happen if the alpha particle occasionally came very close to a small, massive, positively charged nucleus, which repelled it strongly via the Coulomb force.', 'Atomic and nuclear structure', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'An alpha particle of kinetic energy 5.30 MeV travels directly toward a stationary lead nucleus ($Z=82$). Assuming the lead nucleus remains fixed, what is the closest distance of approach of the alpha particle to the nucleus?', '["$4.46\\times10^{-14}$ m", "$2.23\\times10^{-14}$ m", "$8.92\\times10^{-14}$ m", "$4.46\\times10^{-16}$ m"]'::jsonb, 0, 'At the closest approach, all the alpha particle''s kinetic energy converts to electrostatic potential energy: $E_k=\dfrac{(2e)(Ze)}{4\pi\varepsilon_0 r}$, so $r=\dfrac{2Ze^2}{4\pi\varepsilon_0 E_k}=\dfrac{2(82)(1.6\times10^{-19})^2}{4\pi(8.854\times10^{-12})(5.30\times10^6\times1.6\times10^{-19})}\approx4.46\times10^{-14}$ m. This calculation assumes the inverse-square Coulomb force holds all the way in and that the alpha particle travels in a straight line toward the nucleus (a head-on collision). Halving or doubling this value are common factor-of-2 slips, and $4.46\times10^{-16}$ m results from a misplaced decimal in the energy conversion from MeV to joules.', 'Atomic and nuclear structure', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A hydrogen atom''s electron drops from the $n=4$ energy level directly to the $n=2$ energy level. Given that $E_n = -\dfrac{13.6}{n^2}$ eV, what is the energy of the photon emitted in this transition?', '["2.55 eV", "0.85 eV", "3.40 eV", "10.2 eV"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The energies are $E_4=-13.6/16=-0.85$ eV and $E_2=-13.6/4=-3.4$ eV. The photon energy equals the magnitude of the energy released: $E_4-E_2=(-0.85)-(-3.4)=2.55$ eV. Using $E_4$ alone (0.85 eV, sign ignored) or $E_2$ alone (3.40 eV) are common errors from forgetting to take the difference, and 10.2 eV is the energy for the $n=2\to n=1$ transition instead.', 'Atomic and nuclear structure', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'What is the ionization energy required to remove an electron from a hydrogen atom that is already in its $n=3$ excited state? (Use $E_n = -\dfrac{13.6}{n^2}$ eV.)', '["1.51 eV", "13.6 eV", "4.53 eV", "0.85 eV"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Ionization means raising the electron''s energy from $E_3=-13.6/9\approx-1.51$ eV to the free-electron level, $E_\infty=0$. The required energy is $E_\infty-E_3=0-(-1.51)=1.51$ eV. Using the ground-state ionization energy (13.6 eV) ignores that the electron is already excited, and the other distractors come from using the wrong power of $n$ in the denominator.', 'Atomic and nuclear structure', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A hydrogen atom''s electron transitions from the $n=2$ level to the ground state ($n=1$), emitting a photon. What is the wavelength of the emitted photon? (Use $E_n=-13.6/n^2$ eV, $h=6.63\times10^{-34}$ Js, $c=3.0\times10^8$ m/s)', '["$1.22\\times10^{-7}$ m", "$2.43\\times10^{-7}$ m", "$6.08\\times10^{-8}$ m", "$1.22\\times10^{-6}$ m"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The transition energy is $E_2-E_1=(-3.4)-(-13.6)=10.2$ eV $=1.63\times10^{-18}$ J. The wavelength is $\lambda=\dfrac{hc}{E}=\dfrac{(6.63\times10^{-34})(3.0\times10^8)}{1.63\times10^{-18}}\approx1.22\times10^{-7}$ m, which lies in the ultraviolet (this is the Lyman-alpha line). Doubling this comes from a missing factor of 2, halving it from an extra factor of 2, and $1.22\times10^{-6}$ m from a misplaced decimal point in the energy conversion.', 'Atomic and nuclear structure', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'The Lyman series of hydrogen''s spectral lines corresponds to electron transitions that end at a particular energy level, and this series lies in a specific region of the electromagnetic spectrum. Which combination correctly identifies both?', '["Transitions ending at $n=1$; ultraviolet region", "Transitions ending at $n=2$; visible region", "Transitions ending at $n=3$; infrared region", "Transitions ending at $n=1$; infrared region"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Lyman series consists of all transitions from higher energy levels down to the ground state, $n=1$. Because these transitions involve large energy differences, the emitted photons have high frequencies and short wavelengths, placing the entire Lyman series in the ultraviolet region of the spectrum. In contrast, the Balmer series ends at $n=2$ (partly visible) and the Paschen series ends at $n=3$ (infrared).', 'Atomic and nuclear structure', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'In an X-ray tube, electrons are accelerated through a potential difference of 40 kV before striking the target. What is the minimum possible wavelength of the X-rays produced?', '["$3.10\\times10^{-11}$ m", "$1.55\\times10^{-11}$ m", "$6.20\\times10^{-11}$ m", "$3.10\\times10^{-9}$ m"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The shortest-wavelength (most energetic) X-ray photon is produced when an electron loses all of its kinetic energy in a single collision, so $eV=\dfrac{hc}{\lambda_{min}}$, giving $\lambda_{min}=\dfrac{hc}{eV}=\dfrac{(6.63\times10^{-34})(3.0\times10^8)}{(1.6\times10^{-19})(40000)}\approx3.10\times10^{-11}$ m. Halving this comes from an extra factor of 2, doubling it from a missing factor, and $3.10\times10^{-9}$ m from mistakenly using 400 V instead of 40 kV.', 'Atomic and nuclear structure', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'The K absorption edge of a particular element corresponds to a photon wavelength of 50 pm. What is the energy associated with this wavelength, in keV?', '["24.8 keV", "12.4 keV", "49.6 keV", "2.48 keV"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The photon energy is $E=\dfrac{hc}{\lambda}=\dfrac{(6.63\times10^{-34})(3.0\times10^8)}{50\times10^{-12}}\approx3.97\times10^{-15}$ J, which converts to $\dfrac{3.97\times10^{-15}}{1.6\times10^{-19}}\approx24800$ eV, i.e. about 24.8 keV. Halving this (12.4 keV) or doubling it (49.6 keV) come from factor-of-2 slips, and 2.48 keV results from a misplaced decimal point when converting from eV to keV.', 'Atomic and nuclear structure', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'Two nuclides are described as isotopes of the same element. What must be true of them?', '["They have the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons", "They have the same number of neutrons but different numbers of protons", "They have the same mass number but different atomic numbers", "They have identical numbers of protons and neutrons but different chemical properties"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The atomic number, equal to the number of protons, determines which element an atom belongs to and is therefore the same for all isotopes of that element. Isotopes differ in their number of neutrons, which changes the mass number (total nucleon count) while leaving the chemical properties, which depend on the number of protons and hence electrons, essentially unchanged.', 'Atomic and nuclear structure', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A nuclide is represented as $^{23}_{11}\text{Na}$. How many protons and how many neutrons does this nucleus contain?', '["11 protons and 12 neutrons", "23 protons and 11 neutrons", "11 protons and 23 neutrons", "12 protons and 11 neutrons"]'::jsonb, 0, 'In the notation $^{A}_{Z}X$, the subscript $Z$ is the atomic number (number of protons), and the superscript $A$ is the mass number (total number of protons and neutrons). Here $Z=11$ protons, and the number of neutrons is $A-Z=23-11=12$. Confusing $A$ with the proton count, or subtracting in the wrong order, leads to the other incorrect answers.', 'Atomic and nuclear structure', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A proton has a rest mass of $1.67\times10^{-27}$ kg. What is its rest energy, expressed in MeV?', '["938 MeV", "469 MeV", "1876 MeV", "938 keV"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The rest energy is given by Einstein''s relation $E=mc^2=(1.67\times10^{-27})(3.0\times10^8)^2\approx1.50\times10^{-10}$ J. Converting to electron-volts, $E=\dfrac{1.50\times10^{-10}}{1.6\times10^{-19}}\approx9.38\times10^8$ eV $=938$ MeV. Halving this (469 MeV) comes from forgetting to square the speed of light, doubling it (1876 MeV) from an extra factor of 2, and 938 keV from a unit-conversion slip of three orders of magnitude.', 'Atomic and nuclear structure', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A proton (rest mass $1.67\times10^{-27}$ kg, rest energy 938 MeV) moves at a speed of $0.5c$. What is its kinetic energy, using the relativistic expression $KE=(\gamma-1)mc^2$ where $\gamma=\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{1-v^2/c^2}}$?', '["145 MeV", "117 MeV", "938 MeV", "72.5 MeV"]'::jsonb, 0, 'At $v=0.5c$, the Lorentz factor is $\gamma=\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{1-0.25}}=\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{0.75}}\approx1.155$. The kinetic energy is $KE=(\gamma-1)mc^2\approx(0.155)(938)\approx145$ MeV. Using the non-relativistic formula $KE=\frac12 mv^2=\frac12 m(0.5c)^2=0.125mc^2\approx117$ MeV instead noticeably underestimates the true value at this speed, since the classical formula breaks down as $v$ approaches $c$; using the total relativistic energy $\gamma mc^2\approx1083$ MeV (not shown here as 938 MeV, which is only the rest energy) or half the correct value (72.5 MeV) are other common errors.', 'Atomic and nuclear structure', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'In the production of characteristic X-rays, an incident electron knocks an electron out of the innermost (K) shell of a target atom, and an electron from the L shell falls in to fill the vacancy. What is the resulting emitted radiation called, and how is its energy determined?', '["The $K_\\alpha$ line; its energy equals the difference between the L-shell and K-shell energy levels", "The $K_\\alpha$ line; its energy equals the sum of the L-shell and K-shell energy levels", "Bremsstrahlung; its energy depends only on the accelerating voltage of the tube", "The $L_\\alpha$ line; its energy equals the K-shell binding energy alone"]'::jsonb, 0, 'When an L-shell electron drops down to fill a vacancy in the K shell, the photon emitted is called the $K_\alpha$ line, and by conservation of energy its photon energy equals the difference between the two discrete atomic energy levels involved, $h\nu_\alpha=E_K-E_L$ (in magnitude). This sharp-line characteristic spectrum is distinct from the continuous bremsstrahlung spectrum, which arises from the deceleration of electrons in the Coulomb field of the nuclei and depends on the accelerating voltage rather than discrete atomic levels.', 'Atomic and nuclear structure', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'Which of the following statements about X-rays is correct?', '["X-rays are electromagnetic waves and are therefore not deflected by electric or magnetic fields", "X-rays are streams of charged particles and are therefore deflected by magnetic fields", "X-rays cannot ionize matter because they carry no charge", "X-rays have longer wavelengths than visible light"]'::jsonb, 0, 'X-rays are a form of electromagnetic radiation, just like visible light but of much shorter wavelength and higher energy. Being uncharged waves rather than charged particles, they are not deflected by electric or magnetic fields, unlike alpha or beta particles. Despite carrying no charge, X-ray photons can still ionize atoms by transferring energy to bound electrons, and their wavelengths (roughly 0.01 to 10 nm) are far shorter than those of visible light.', 'Atomic and nuclear structure', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'An electron in a hydrogen atom''s ground state, orbiting at the Bohr radius, is described as occupying a stable, non-radiating orbit. Which of Bohr''s postulates justifies why the electron does not continuously lose energy and spiral into the nucleus, in spite of classical electromagnetic theory predicting that an accelerating charge should radiate energy?', '["Bohr postulated that electrons can move in certain discrete, stationary orbits without radiating energy, as long as their angular momentum is an integer multiple of $h/2\\pi$", "Bohr postulated that the electron''s charge is neutralized by the nucleus, so no acceleration occurs", "Bohr postulated that electrons move so fast that classical electromagnetism no longer applies to them at all", "Bohr postulated that the electron only radiates energy when it is in the ground state"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Classically, an orbiting (and hence centripetally accelerating) electron should continuously radiate electromagnetic energy and spiral into the nucleus within a fraction of a second, which contradicts the observed stability of atoms. Bohr resolved this by postulating that only certain discrete orbits, in which the electron''s orbital angular momentum equals an integer multiple of $h/2\pi$, are allowed as stationary states, and while occupying one of these orbits, the electron simply does not radiate, regardless of classical predictions.', 'Atomic and nuclear structure', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A metal surface is illuminated by light of a fixed frequency above the surface''s threshold value. If the intensity of the light is increased while the frequency is kept the same, what happens to the maximum kinetic energy of the emitted electrons?', '["It increases in proportion to the intensity", "It stays the same, but more electrons are emitted per second", "It decreases because more electrons compete for the same energy", "It becomes zero because the surface is over-saturated"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The maximum kinetic energy of photoelectrons is fixed by $\frac{1}{2}mv^2 = hf - \phi$, which depends only on the frequency $f$ and the work function $\phi$, not on the intensity of the light. Increasing the intensity increases the number of photons arriving per second, and hence the number of electrons ejected per second, while the maximum kinetic energy per electron is unchanged.', 'Wave-particle duality and the photoelectric effect', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'For photoelectric emission to occur at all from a given metal surface, what condition must the incident light satisfy?', '["Its intensity must exceed a minimum threshold value", "Its frequency must exceed a threshold value characteristic of the metal", "Its wavelength must exceed a threshold value characteristic of the metal", "It must be incident for longer than a minimum threshold time"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Each metal has a threshold frequency $f_0$, set by its work function through $\phi = hf_0$, below which no photoelectrons are emitted no matter how intense the light is. Above $f_0$, emission begins immediately, with no dependence on exposure time.', 'Wave-particle duality and the photoelectric effect', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'In the photoelectric effect, experiments show no measurable delay between a surface being irradiated and electrons being ejected from it, even when the light source is very faint. Why did classical wave theory struggle to explain this observation?', '["Wave theory predicted electrons could never be ejected from a metal surface", "Wave theory required an electron to gradually accumulate energy from the wavefront it intercepts, which should take a measurable time for a very weak source", "Wave theory predicted electrons would be ejected before the light even arrived", "Wave theory predicted the delay would depend only on the electron''s mass"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under the wave picture, the energy of a feeble light source is spread continuously over the wavefront, so an individual electron would need to absorb energy from a vanishingly small intercepted portion of the wave, which should require a noticeable accumulation time. The instantaneous emission actually observed instead matches a picture in which the electron absorbs one whole photon''s energy at once.', 'Wave-particle duality and the photoelectric effect', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A copper surface is illuminated by radiation of wavelength $2537 \, \text{\AA}$ and the stopping potential is measured to be $0.24 \, \text{V}$. How much energy does an electron expend escaping through the copper surface?', '["$0.24 \\, \\text{eV}$", "$2.44 \\, \\text{eV}$", "$4.66 \\, \\text{eV}$", "$9.32 \\, \\text{eV}$"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The incident photon energy is $E = hc/\lambda = (6.626\times10^{-34} \times 3\times10^8)/(2537\times10^{-10}) \, \text{J} \approx 4.90 \, \text{eV}$. Since the stopping potential gives the maximum kinetic energy of the emitted electrons as $0.24 \, \text{eV}$, the energy spent escaping the surface (the work function) is $4.90 - 0.24 \approx 4.66 \, \text{eV}$.', 'Wave-particle duality and the photoelectric effect', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A copper surface has a work function of $4.66 \, \text{eV}$. What is the threshold wavelength for photoelectric emission from this surface?', '["$1334 \\, \\text{\\AA}$", "$2668 \\, \\text{\\AA}$", "$4004 \\, \\text{\\AA}$", "$5336 \\, \\text{\\AA}$"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The threshold wavelength satisfies $\phi = hc/\lambda_0$, so $\lambda_0 = hc/\phi = (6.626\times10^{-34}\times 3\times10^8)/(4.66\times1.6\times10^{-19}) \approx 2.67\times10^{-7} \, \text{m} = 2668 \, \text{\AA}$.', 'Wave-particle duality and the photoelectric effect', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'Electrons are ejected from a copper surface with a maximum kinetic energy of $0.24 \, \text{eV}$. What is the speed of the most energetic ejected electrons? (electron mass $= 9.11\times10^{-31} \, \text{kg}$)', '["$2.9\\times10^{4} \\, \\text{m/s}$", "$2.9\\times10^{5} \\, \\text{m/s}$", "$2.9\\times10^{6} \\, \\text{m/s}$", "$5.8\\times10^{5} \\, \\text{m/s}$"]'::jsonb, 1, 'From $\frac{1}{2}mv^2 = 0.24 \times 1.6\times10^{-19} \, \text{J}$, $v = \sqrt{2\times0.24\times1.6\times10^{-19}/9.11\times10^{-31}} \approx 2.9\times10^{5} \, \text{m/s}$.', 'Wave-particle duality and the photoelectric effect', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'Photoelectrons emitted from a sodium surface exposed to light of wavelength $4.0\times10^{-7} \, \text{m}$ have a maximum kinetic energy of $0.8 \, \text{eV}$. What is the work function of the surface?', '["$1.51 \\, \\text{eV}$", "$2.31 \\, \\text{eV}$", "$3.11 \\, \\text{eV}$", "$3.91 \\, \\text{eV}$"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The photon energy is $E = hc/\lambda \approx 3.11 \, \text{eV}$. The work function is $\phi = E - KE_{max} = 3.11 - 0.8 \approx 2.31 \, \text{eV}$, matching the accepted work function of sodium.', 'Wave-particle duality and the photoelectric effect', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'Photoelectric emission from a certain metal surface ceases when the incident wavelength exceeds $5.6\times10^{-7} \, \text{m}$. What is the maximum kinetic energy, in eV, of electrons emitted when the surface is illuminated with light of wavelength $4.2\times10^{-7} \, \text{m}$?', '["$0.24 \\, \\text{eV}$", "$0.74 \\, \\text{eV}$", "$1.48 \\, \\text{eV}$", "$2.22 \\, \\text{eV}$"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The work function is $\phi = hc/\lambda_0 = hc/(5.6\times10^{-7}) \approx 2.22 \, \text{eV}$. The incident photon energy is $hc/(4.2\times10^{-7}) \approx 2.96 \, \text{eV}$, so $KE_{max} = 2.96 - 2.22 \approx 0.74 \, \text{eV}$.', 'Wave-particle duality and the photoelectric effect', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'Photoelectrons of kinetic energy $1.5 \, \text{eV}$ are ejected from a tungsten surface whose threshold wavelength for photoelectric emission is $2.3\times10^{-7} \, \text{m}$. What is the wavelength of the incident light?', '["$1.8\\times10^{-7} \\, \\text{m}$", "$2.3\\times10^{-7} \\, \\text{m}$", "$3.8\\times10^{-7} \\, \\text{m}$", "$5.8\\times10^{-7} \\, \\text{m}$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The tungsten work function is $\phi = hc/\lambda_0 \approx 5.40 \, \text{eV}$. The incident photon energy must be $E = \phi + KE_{max} \approx 6.90 \, \text{eV}$, so $\lambda = hc/E \approx 1.8\times10^{-7} \, \text{m}$.', 'Wave-particle duality and the photoelectric effect', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A beam of X-rays is Compton-scattered through $45^\circ$ by free electrons. If the wavelength of the scattered beam is $0.35 \, \text{\AA}$, what was the wavelength of the incident beam? (Compton wavelength of the electron $h/m_ec = 0.02426 \, \text{\AA}$)', '["$0.0071 \\, \\text{\\AA}$", "$0.3429 \\, \\text{\\AA}$", "$0.3571 \\, \\text{\\AA}$", "$0.4300 \\, \\text{\\AA}$"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Compton shift is $\Delta\lambda = \frac{h}{m_ec}(1-\cos\theta) = 0.02426(1-\cos45^\circ) \approx 0.0071 \, \text{\AA}$. Since the scattered wavelength is always the larger one, the incident wavelength is $\lambda = \lambda'' - \Delta\lambda \approx 0.35 - 0.0071 \approx 0.3429 \, \text{\AA}$.', 'Wave-particle duality and the photoelectric effect', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'An X-ray photon of initial frequency $3\times10^{19} \, \text{Hz}$ Compton-scatters off an electron and is deflected through $90^\circ$. What is its new frequency?', '["$0.4\\times10^{19} \\, \\text{Hz}$", "$2.4\\times10^{19} \\, \\text{Hz}$", "$5.4\\times10^{19} \\, \\text{Hz}$", "$9.4\\times10^{19} \\, \\text{Hz}$"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The initial wavelength is $\lambda_0 = c/f_0 = 1.0\times10^{-11} \, \text{m}$. For $\theta = 90^\circ$, $\Delta\lambda = h/m_ec \approx 2.42\times10^{-12} \, \text{m}$, giving a scattered wavelength of $\approx 1.24\times10^{-11} \, \text{m}$, so the new frequency is $f = c/\lambda \approx 2.4\times10^{19} \, \text{Hz}$. Compton scattering always lengthens the wavelength, so the frequency decreases.', 'Wave-particle duality and the photoelectric effect', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'X-rays of wavelength $1 \, \text{\AA}$ are Compton-scattered from a carbon target. What is the largest possible wavelength change in the scattered radiation?', '["$0.0000 \\, \\text{\\AA}$", "$0.0243 \\, \\text{\\AA}$", "$0.0485 \\, \\text{\\AA}$", "$0.0971 \\, \\text{\\AA}$"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The Compton shift $\Delta\lambda = \frac{h}{m_ec}(1-\cos\theta)$ is largest when $\theta = 180^\circ$, giving $\Delta\lambda_{max} = 2h/m_ec \approx 2\times0.02426 \approx 0.0485 \, \text{\AA}$, independent of the incident wavelength.', 'Wave-particle duality and the photoelectric effect', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'In Compton scattering of X-rays from loosely-bound outer electrons in a target such as carbon, the scattered radiation is observed to contain two components: one at the same wavelength as the incident beam (the unmodified line) and one at a longer wavelength (the modified line). What accounts for the unmodified line?', '["Photons that pass straight through the target without any interaction at all", "Scattering off tightly-bound inner-shell electrons, which recoil together with the whole atom so that the effective scattering mass is very large and the wavelength shift becomes negligible", "A separate beam of visible light produced as a byproduct of ionisation", "Photons that gain energy from the target rather than losing it"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Compton wavelength shift depends inversely on the mass of the particle the photon recoils against. When the photon scatters from an electron tightly bound to a heavy atom, the atom as a whole absorbs the recoil, and because atomic masses are thousands of times larger than an electron''s mass, the resulting shift is too small to detect, producing the unmodified line.', 'Wave-particle duality and the photoelectric effect', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'According to the de Broglie hypothesis, what determines the wavelength associated with a moving material particle such as an electron?', '["Its kinetic energy alone, through $\\lambda = 2T/h$", "Its momentum, through $\\lambda = h/p$", "Its charge-to-mass ratio, through $\\lambda = h(q/m)$", "Its speed alone, independent of its mass"]'::jsonb, 1, 'De Broglie proposed that every material particle has an associated wavelength $\lambda = h/p$, where $p$ is its momentum, in direct analogy with the photon relation between momentum and wavelength for radiation.', 'Wave-particle duality and the photoelectric effect', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'What is the de Broglie wavelength associated with an electron of kinetic energy $216 \, \text{eV}$? (electron mass $= 9.11\times10^{-31} \, \text{kg}$, treat the electron non-relativistically)', '["$4.2\\times10^{-11} \\, \\text{m}$", "$8.3\\times10^{-11} \\, \\text{m}$", "$1.7\\times10^{-10} \\, \\text{m}$", "$8.3\\times10^{-9} \\, \\text{m}$"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Momentum is $p = \sqrt{2mT} = \sqrt{2\times9.11\times10^{-31}\times216\times1.6\times10^{-19}} \approx 7.94\times10^{-24} \, \text{kg m/s}$, so $\lambda = h/p \approx 8.3\times10^{-11} \, \text{m}$.', 'Wave-particle duality and the photoelectric effect', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'In 1927-1928, Davisson and Germer, and separately G.P. Thomson, passed beams of electrons through crystalline targets and observed diffraction patterns of concentric rings, similar to X-ray diffraction patterns. What did this experiment confirm?', '["That electrons carry no charge under certain conditions", "That electrons exhibit wave-like behaviour, with a wavelength consistent with the de Broglie relation", "That the electron has an internal quark structure", "That electrons travel faster than the speed of light in a crystal lattice"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The diffraction rings could only be explained if the electron beam behaved as a wave interacting with the periodic spacing of the crystal lattice, and the wavelength extracted from the ring pattern matched the value predicted by $\lambda = h/p$, confirming the wave nature of matter.', 'Wave-particle duality and the photoelectric effect', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'The Heisenberg uncertainty principle places a fundamental limit on how precisely which pair of quantities can be simultaneously known for a particle?', '["Charge and mass", "Position and momentum", "Energy and charge", "Spin and charge"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The uncertainty principle states that $\Delta x \cdot \Delta p \approx \hbar$: the product of the uncertainties in position and momentum cannot be reduced below a value of order Planck''s constant, no matter how precise the measuring apparatus.', 'Wave-particle duality and the photoelectric effect', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'An electron''s speed is known with an uncertainty of $\Delta v = 10^{5} \, \text{m/s}$. Using the Heisenberg uncertainty principle, estimate the minimum uncertainty in its position. (electron mass $= 9.11\times10^{-31} \, \text{kg}$)', '["$1.2\\times10^{-9} \\, \\text{m}$", "$1.2\\times10^{-10} \\, \\text{m}$", "$5.8\\times10^{-9} \\, \\text{m}$", "$1.2\\times10^{-8} \\, \\text{m}$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Using $\Delta x \approx \frac{\hbar}{m\Delta v}$ with $\hbar = h/2\pi \approx 1.055\times10^{-34} \, \text{J s}$, $\Delta x \approx 1.055\times10^{-34}/(9.11\times10^{-31}\times10^{5}) \approx 1.2\times10^{-9} \, \text{m}$.', 'Wave-particle duality and the photoelectric effect', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'The uncertainty principle helps explain why an electron in an atom cannot simply spiral into the nucleus under the attractive Coulomb force. What is the physical reasoning?', '["Confining the electron to a very small region near the nucleus would force a very large uncertainty, and hence expected magnitude, in its momentum, giving it enough kinetic energy to resist collapse", "The nucleus repels electrons through the strong nuclear force", "Electrons have zero mass, so no force can accelerate them inward", "The Coulomb force switches sign at very short range"]'::jsonb, 0, 'If the electron were confined to a very small position uncertainty $\Delta x$ near the nucleus, the uncertainty principle requires a correspondingly large momentum uncertainty $\Delta p \sim \hbar/\Delta x$. This implies a large expected kinetic energy that opposes further confinement, producing an effective equilibrium instead of collapse into the nucleus.', 'Wave-particle duality and the photoelectric effect', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A nucleus is denoted $^{270}_{107}X$. How many neutrons does this nucleus contain?', '["107", "163", "270", "377"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The mass number $A = 270$ is the total number of nucleons, and the subscript $Z = 107$ is the number of protons. The number of neutrons is $N = A - Z = 270 - 107 = 163$.', 'Atomic and nuclear structure', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'Which of the following particles is identical to a doubly-ionised helium nucleus?', '["A gamma particle", "An alpha particle", "A beta particle", "An X-ray photon"]'::jsonb, 1, 'An alpha particle consists of two protons and two neutrons bound together, which is exactly the composition of a helium-4 nucleus stripped of its electrons.', 'Radioactivity, fission, fusion and nuclear reactors', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'The binding energy of a nucleus is best described as the energy required to do what?', '["Accelerate the nucleus to relativistic speed", "Assemble the nucleus from a lighter, related nuclide", "Separate the nucleus completely into its individual free protons and neutrons", "Prevent the nucleus from ever decaying"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Binding energy is defined as the energy that would have to be supplied to pull a nucleus completely apart into its constituent free nucleons; equivalently, it is the energy released when those free nucleons come together to form the nucleus.', 'Atomic and nuclear structure', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A 10 g sample of a radioactive material is left undisturbed for a period equal to five of its half-lives. How much of the original material remains?', '["0.625 g", "1.25 g", "0.313 g", "5.00 g"]'::jsonb, 2, 'After $n$ half-lives, a fraction $(1/2)^n$ of the original sample remains. For $n=5$: $10 \times (1/2)^5 = 10/32 \approx 0.313 \, \text{g}$.', 'Radioactivity, fission, fusion and nuclear reactors', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A sequence of radioactive decays, where each daughter nucleus is itself radioactive and decays further, is called a radioactive decay series. What must such a series eventually terminate in?', '["A stable, non-radioactive nucleus", "Pure hydrogen", "A nucleus that has lost all of its protons", "An endless loop back to the original parent nucleus"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Since each step in the series removes energy from the nucleus through emitted radiation, the sequence of decays must eventually reach a nuclide with no energetically favourable decay path available, that is, a stable nucleus.', 'Radioactivity, fission, fusion and nuclear reactors', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'In virtually all beta-minus decay processes, a neutron converts into a proton and an electron is emitted. What other particle is emitted along with the electron?', '["An antineutrino", "A neutrino", "An alpha particle", "A gamma-ray photon"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Beta-minus decay is $n \rightarrow p + e^- + \bar\nu$: alongside the emitted electron, an antineutrino is emitted so that lepton number, energy and momentum are all properly conserved.', 'Radioactivity, fission, fusion and nuclear reactors', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'Polonium-214 ($^{214}_{84}Po$) undergoes alpha decay. What is the resulting daughter nucleus?', '["$^{214}_{82}Pb$", "$^{210}_{82}Pb$", "$^{210}_{86}Rn$", "$^{218}_{86}Rn$"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Alpha decay removes 2 protons and 2 neutrons: $A = 214 - 4 = 210$ and $Z = 84 - 2 = 82$, which is lead, giving $^{210}_{82}Pb$.', 'Radioactivity, fission, fusion and nuclear reactors', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'Uranium-238 ($^{238}_{92}U$) undergoes alpha decay. What is the resulting daughter nucleus?', '["$^{234}_{90}Th$", "$^{234}_{93}Np$", "$^{238}_{90}Th$", "$^{242}_{94}Pu$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Alpha decay reduces the mass number by 4 and the atomic number by 2: $A = 238-4 = 234$, $Z = 92-2 = 90$, which corresponds to thorium, $^{234}_{90}Th$.', 'Radioactivity, fission, fusion and nuclear reactors', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A nitrogen-14 nucleus ($^{14}_{7}N$) absorbs a deuterium nucleus ($^{2}_{1}H$) to form a compound nucleus. What are the name, atomic number and mass number of the resulting compound nucleus?', '["Carbon-16, $Z=6$, $A=16$", "Oxygen-16, $Z=8$, $A=16$", "Oxygen-12, $Z=8$, $A=12$", "Carbon-12, $Z=6$, $A=12$"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Nucleon number and charge must both be conserved: $A = 14+2 = 16$ and $Z = 7+1 = 8$. The element with 8 protons is oxygen, so the compound nucleus is oxygen-16.', 'Radioactivity, fission, fusion and nuclear reactors', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'Thorium-232 ($^{232}_{90}Th$) decays by alpha emission to a daughter nucleus, which itself then undergoes beta-minus decay. What is the final nucleus produced?', '["$^{228}_{88}Ra$", "$^{228}_{89}Ac$", "$^{228}_{91}Pa$", "$^{236}_{92}U$"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Alpha decay gives $A=232-4=228$, $Z=90-2=88$ (radium-228). The subsequent beta-minus decay increases the atomic number by 1 without changing the mass number: $Z=88+1=89$, $A=228$, which is actinium, $^{228}_{89}Ac$.', 'Radioactivity, fission, fusion and nuclear reactors', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A sample of $0.1 \, \text{mg}$ of caesium-137 (molar mass approximately $137 \, \text{g/mol}$, half-life $28$ years) is prepared. What is its activity, approximately, in becquerels?', '["$3.4\\times10^{5} \\, \\text{Bq}$", "$3.4\\times10^{8} \\, \\text{Bq}$", "$3.4\\times10^{11} \\, \\text{Bq}$", "$3.4\\times10^{16} \\, \\text{Bq}$"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The number of atoms is $N_0 = (m/M)N_A = (10^{-4}\text{g}/137\text{g/mol}) \times 6.022\times10^{23} \approx 4.40\times10^{17}$. The decay constant is $\lambda = \ln 2 / t_{1/2} = \ln 2/(28\times3.156\times10^7 \, \text{s}) \approx 7.84\times10^{-10} \, \text{s}^{-1}$. The activity is $A = \lambda N_0 \approx 3.4\times10^{8} \, \text{Bq}$.', 'Radioactivity, fission, fusion and nuclear reactors', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'In a certain fission reaction, a neutron is captured by uranium-235 ($M = 235.043915 \, \text{u}$) and the nucleus splits into barium-141 ($M = 140.9139 \, \text{u}$), krypton-92 ($M = 91.8973 \, \text{u}$) and three neutrons ($m_n = 1.008665 \, \text{u}$ each). Approximately how much energy is released in this single fission event?', '["$17.6 \\, \\text{MeV}$", "$100.3 \\, \\text{MeV}$", "$200.6 \\, \\text{MeV}$", "$931.5 \\, \\text{MeV}$"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The mass defect is $\Delta m = (m_n + M_{U235}) - (M_{Ba} + M_{Kr} + 3m_n) \approx 0.2154 \, \text{u}$. Using $1 \, \text{u} = 931.494 \, \text{MeV}$, the energy released is $Q = \Delta m \times 931.494 \approx 200.6 \, \text{MeV}$, consistent with the well-known value of roughly 200 MeV released per uranium-235 fission.', 'Radioactivity, fission, fusion and nuclear reactors', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A helium-4 nucleus can, in principle, be broken down into two separate hydrogen atoms and two free neutrons. Using atomic mass values $m_H = 1.007825 \, \text{u}$, $m_n = 1.008665 \, \text{u}$ and $m_{He4} = 4.002603 \, \text{u}$, what is the binding energy of the helium-4 nucleus?', '["$2.83 \\, \\text{MeV}$", "$7.07 \\, \\text{MeV}$", "$28.3 \\, \\text{MeV}$", "$931.5 \\, \\text{MeV}$"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The mass defect is $\Delta m = (2m_H + 2m_n) - m_{He4} = (2\times1.007825 + 2\times1.008665) - 4.002603 \approx 0.030377 \, \text{u}$. The binding energy is $\Delta m \times 931.494 \, \text{MeV/u} \approx 28.3 \, \text{MeV}$.', 'Atomic and nuclear structure', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'Two isotopes of hydrogen, of atomic masses $2.014102 \, \text{u}$ and $3.016049 \, \text{u}$, fuse to form a helium-4 nucleus ($4.002603 \, \text{u}$) and a free neutron ($1.008665 \, \text{u}$). Approximately how much energy is released in this fusion reaction?', '["$1.76 \\, \\text{MeV}$", "$17.6 \\, \\text{MeV}$", "$176 \\, \\text{MeV}$", "$200.6 \\, \\text{MeV}$"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The mass defect is $\Delta m = (2.014102 + 3.016049) - (4.002603+1.008665) \approx 0.018883 \, \text{u}$, giving $Q = 0.018883\times931.494 \approx 17.6 \, \text{MeV}$, in agreement with the known energy release for deuterium-tritium fusion.', 'Radioactivity, fission, fusion and nuclear reactors', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'How many protons and how many neutrons does the tin isotope $^{120}_{50}Sn$ contain?', '["50 protons, 50 neutrons", "50 protons, 70 neutrons", "70 protons, 50 neutrons", "60 protons, 60 neutrons"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The subscript gives the number of protons directly, $Z=50$. The number of neutrons is $N = A - Z = 120 - 50 = 70$.', 'Atomic and nuclear structure', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A sample of strontium-90, which has a half-life of $28.8$ years, is stored. Approximately how long will it take for two-thirds of the original sample to decay away?', '["$18.2$ years", "$28.8$ years", "$45.6$ years", "$86.4$ years"]'::jsonb, 2, 'If two-thirds decays away, one-third remains, so $N/N_0 = 1/3$. From $N = N_0 e^{-\lambda t}$ with $\lambda = \ln 2/28.8 \, \text{yr}^{-1}$, $t = -\ln(1/3)/\lambda \approx 45.6 \, \text{years}$.', 'Radioactivity, fission, fusion and nuclear reactors', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A radioactive isotope is observed such that, after 2 hours, only one-eighth of the original sample remains. What is the half-life of this isotope?', '["$15 \\, \\text{minutes}$", "$20 \\, \\text{minutes}$", "$40 \\, \\text{minutes}$", "$60 \\, \\text{minutes}$"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Since $(1/2)^n = 1/8$ requires $n=3$ half-lives to have elapsed in 2 hours, each half-life is $2\,\text{hr}/3 = 0.667 \, \text{hr} = 40 \, \text{minutes}$.', 'Radioactivity, fission, fusion and nuclear reactors', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'Radium-226, initially at rest, decays by emitting a $4.706 \, \text{MeV}$ alpha particle. Using conservation of momentum and treating the daughter nucleus (mass number 222) and the alpha particle (mass $4.002603 \, \text{u}$) non-relativistically, what is the recoil speed of the daughter nucleus?', '["$6.8\\times10^{4} \\, \\text{m/s}$", "$2.7\\times10^{5} \\, \\text{m/s}$", "$1.5\\times10^{7} \\, \\text{m/s}$", "$3.0\\times10^{7} \\, \\text{m/s}$"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The alpha particle''s speed follows from $\frac{1}{2}m_\alpha v_\alpha^2 = 4.706 \, \text{MeV}$, giving $v_\alpha \approx 1.51\times10^{7} \, \text{m/s}$. Since the parent nucleus starts at rest, momentum conservation requires $m_\alpha v_\alpha = m_{daughter} v_{daughter}$, so $v_{daughter} = (m_\alpha/m_{daughter})v_\alpha \approx (4/222)\times 1.51\times10^7 \approx 2.7\times10^{5} \, \text{m/s}$.', 'Radioactivity, fission, fusion and nuclear reactors', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'The iodine isotope used in medical thyroid tests has a half-life of 8 days. If a patient''s thyroid gland accumulates $4.0\times10^{22}$ nuclei of this isotope, what is the observed activity 24 hours later?', '["$4.0\\times10^{14} \\, \\text{decays/s}$", "$4.0\\times10^{16} \\, \\text{decays/s}$", "$1.0\\times10^{6} \\, \\text{decays/s}$", "$4.0\\times10^{22} \\, \\text{decays/s}$"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The decay constant is $\lambda = \ln2/(8\times24\times3600 \, \text{s}) \approx 1.0\times10^{-6} \, \text{s}^{-1}$. Since only a small fraction has decayed after 24 hours, the activity is approximately $\lambda N_0 = 1.0\times10^{-6}\times4.0\times10^{22} \approx 4.0\times10^{16} \, \text{decays/s}$.', 'Radioactivity, fission, fusion and nuclear reactors', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'The iodine isotope used in medical thyroid tests has a half-life of 8 days. Starting from $4.0\times10^{22}$ nuclei accumulated in a patient''s thyroid gland, approximately how many of the original nuclei remain after 1 day?', '["$4.0\\times10^{22}$", "$3.7\\times10^{22}$", "$2.0\\times10^{22}$", "$3.7\\times10^{16}$"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Using $N = N_0 e^{-\lambda t}$ with $\lambda \approx 1.0\times10^{-6} \, \text{s}^{-1}$ and $t = 86400 \, \text{s}$, $N \approx 4.0\times10^{22}\times e^{-0.0864} \approx 3.7\times10^{22}$ nuclei.', 'Radioactivity, fission, fusion and nuclear reactors', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A bone fragment from an archaeological site registers a radioactivity count rate of $20$ counts per minute, while a similar sample of fresh bone gives $25$ counts per minute. If the half-life of carbon-14 is $5530$ years, approximately how old is the fragment?', '["$890$ years", "$1780$ years", "$3560$ years", "$5530$ years"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Activity is proportional to the number of remaining radioactive nuclei, so $20 = 25e^{-\lambda t}$ gives $\lambda t = \ln(25/20) \approx 0.223$. With $\lambda = \ln2/5530 \, \text{yr}^{-1}$, $t \approx 0.223/\lambda \approx 1780 \, \text{years}$.', 'Radioactivity, fission, fusion and nuclear reactors', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'A different bone fragment gives a count rate of $15$ counts per minute, while a fresh bone sample gives $19$ counts per minute. Taking the half-life of carbon-14 as $5530$ years, approximately how old is this fragment?', '["$940$ years", "$1886$ years", "$2820$ years", "$3772$ years"]'::jsonb, 1, 'As before, $15 = 19e^{-\lambda t}$ gives $\lambda t = \ln(19/15) \approx 0.236$. With $\lambda = \ln2/5530 \, \text{yr}^{-1}$, $t \approx 0.236/\lambda \approx 1886 \, \text{years}$.', 'Radioactivity, fission, fusion and nuclear reactors', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'Which of the following criteria is used to judge whether a given nuclide is likely to be nuclear stable?', '["All isotopes with proton number greater than 83 are unstable", "All isotopes with an odd number of protons are automatically unstable regardless of neutron number", "Stability requires the neutron number to always be double the proton number", "Only isotopes with a prime mass number can be stable"]'::jsonb, 0, 'One of the general empirical rules for nuclear stability is that every isotope with a proton number $Z > 83$ is unstable; heavier nuclei need an even larger excess of neutrons over protons to remain bound, and eventually no combination is stable.', 'Atomic and nuclear structure', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'Considering the pairing of protons and neutrons in known stable nuclei, which combination is found to be the most common?', '["Even number of protons and even number of neutrons", "Even number of protons and odd number of neutrons", "Odd number of protons and odd number of neutrons", "All combinations occur with roughly equal frequency"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Among the known stable nuclides, the even-proton, even-neutron combination is by far the most common, reflecting the tendency of nucleons to pair up in a way that increases stability; the odd-odd combination is by contrast extremely rare.', 'Atomic and nuclear structure', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'The nuclear force that holds nucleons together in a nucleus is best described as:', '["Long-ranged, acting equally between any two nucleons no matter how far apart", "Short-ranged and strongly attractive, acting only between nucleons that are close neighbours, but between any combination of protons and neutrons", "Attractive only between two protons, and repulsive between two neutrons", "Weaker than both gravity and the electrostatic force between protons"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The strong nuclear force is short-ranged, so a nucleon effectively only interacts with its nearest neighbours, but it is strongly attractive and acts equally between any pair of nucleons, whether proton-proton, proton-neutron or neutron-neutron, which is what allows it to overcome the electrostatic repulsion between protons.', 'Atomic and nuclear structure', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'In the radioactive decay law $N = N_0 e^{-\lambda t}$, what does the constant $\lambda$ represent, and how is it related to the half-life $t_{1/2}$?', '["$\\lambda$ is the decay constant, related to the half-life by $t_{1/2} = 0.693/\\lambda$", "$\\lambda$ is the initial activity, related to the half-life by $t_{1/2} = \\lambda/0.693$", "$\\lambda$ is the atomic mass, unrelated to the half-life", "$\\lambda$ is the binding energy per nucleon, related to the half-life by $t_{1/2} = \\lambda^2$"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The decay constant $\lambda$ gives the probability of decay per unit time for each nucleus. Setting $N=N_0/2$ at $t=t_{1/2}$ in the decay law gives $t_{1/2} = \ln2/\lambda \approx 0.693/\lambda$.', 'Radioactivity, fission, fusion and nuclear reactors', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'The curie (Ci), a traditional unit of radioactivity, is defined as the activity of one gram of radium. What is this activity in SI units (becquerels, where 1 Bq = 1 decay/s)?', '["$3.7\\times10^{2} \\, \\text{Bq}$", "$3.7\\times10^{6} \\, \\text{Bq}$", "$3.7\\times10^{10} \\, \\text{Bq}$", "$1.0\\times10^{10} \\, \\text{Bq}$"]'::jsonb, 2, 'By definition, $1 \, \text{Ci} = 3.70\times10^{10}$ decays per second, which is equivalent to $3.70\times10^{10} \, \text{Bq}$.', 'Radioactivity, fission, fusion and nuclear reactors', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'For a nuclear reaction $A(a,b)B$, the Q-value is defined as the kinetic energy of the products minus the kinetic energy of the reactants. If the Q-value for a particular reaction is negative, what does this indicate?', '["The reaction is exoergic and releases net energy", "The reaction is endoergic and requires a minimum, threshold kinetic energy to proceed", "The reaction violates conservation of charge", "The reaction can proceed spontaneously at any incident energy, however small"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A negative Q-value means the rest mass of the products exceeds that of the reactants, so energy must be supplied for the reaction to occur; such a reaction is endoergic and only proceeds once the incident particle''s kinetic energy exceeds a minimum threshold value.', 'Radioactivity, fission, fusion and nuclear reactors', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'The reaction $^{14}_{7}N(\alpha,p)^{17}_{8}O$ has a Q-value of $-1.193 \, \text{MeV}$, with the incident alpha particle mass $m_\alpha = 4.0002603 \, \text{u}$ and the stationary target mass $M_N = 14.003074 \, \text{u}$. What is the minimum (threshold) kinetic energy the alpha particle must have to initiate this reaction?', '["$1.19 \\, \\text{MeV}$", "$1.53 \\, \\text{MeV}$", "$4.77 \\, \\text{MeV}$", "$14.03 \\, \\text{MeV}$"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The threshold energy is $T_{min} = \left(1+\frac{m_\alpha}{M_N}\right)|Q| = \left(1+\frac{4.0002603}{14.003074}\right)\times1.193 \approx 1.53 \, \text{MeV}$.', 'Radioactivity, fission, fusion and nuclear reactors', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'In a fission chain reaction inside a nuclear reactor, what is meant by the "critical mass" of the fissionable material?', '["The maximum mass of material that can be melted before an explosion occurs", "The minimum mass of fissionable material required so that, on average, enough neutrons induce further fissions to sustain the chain reaction", "The exact mass of material needed to stop all fission reactions instantly", "The mass at which the material becomes electrically neutral"]'::jsonb, 1, 'If the sample is too small, too many neutrons escape from its surface before inducing further fissions, and the chain reaction dies out. The critical mass is the minimum quantity of fissionable material needed so that enough neutrons are captured internally to sustain the reaction at a steady rate.', 'Radioactivity, fission, fusion and nuclear reactors', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'Naturally occurring uranium is composed of about 99.3% uranium-238 and only 0.7% uranium-235, the isotope that readily undergoes fission with slow neutrons. To obtain a workable critical mass for a nuclear reactor, what is typically done to the uranium fuel?', '["It is diluted with additional uranium-238 to reduce its activity", "It is enriched to increase the proportion of uranium-235, typically to a few percent for reactor-grade fuel", "It is cooled to absolute zero to stop all neutron absorption", "It is converted entirely into lead before use"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Because natural uranium-238 readily absorbs neutrons without fissioning, removing them from the chain reaction, the fissionable uranium-235 content must be concentrated, or enriched, typically to 3-5% for electricity-generating reactors (and much higher for weapons-grade material), to reduce the critical mass to a workable size.', 'Radioactivity, fission, fusion and nuclear reactors', false, 'second', 3),
  ('phy-124-electricity-magnetism-atomic', 'PHY 124: Fundamentals of Physics III (Electricity, Magnetism and Atomic Physics)', 'University of Nigeria, Nsukka', 'Faculty of Physical Sciences, Faculty of Engineering', 'Physics and Astronomy', '100 Level', 'Fusion reactions, such as those powering stars including the sun, release energy by combining light nuclei into a heavier one. Compared with a fission reaction using an equivalent mass of fissionable material, why can hydrogen fusion in principle release considerably more total energy per unit mass of fuel?', '["Because hydrogen is radioactive while fissionable isotopes are not", "Because a given mass of hydrogen contains many more nuclei than an equal mass of a heavy fissionable isotope, and no critical mass or chain-reaction material is needed to release their energy", "Because fusion reactions do not obey conservation of mass-energy", "Because fusion only occurs at absolute zero temperature"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Since hydrogen nuclei are much lighter than heavy fissionable nuclei, a given mass of hydrogen contains far more individual nuclei available to react than the same mass of a heavy fissionable isotope, so more nuclear reactions, and hence more energy, can in principle be released per unit mass of fuel.', 'Radioactivity, fission, fusion and nuclear reactors', false, 'second', 3);
